module gunbc.plans.demand_engine_program

import std.dissolution { unbound_dissolution }
import gunbc.plan { Plan, PlanRetiresWhen, PlanIsAuthorityOnly }
import std.markdown { MarkdownBlock, TableBlock, AlignNone }
import gunbc.plans.md_helpers { h2, h3, p, li, ul, ol, cell, row, quote }
import gunbc.plans.demand_restatement_follow_up { demand_restatement_follow_up_commit_range, demand_restatement_retained_row_texts }
import gunbc.design_ruling { project_documentation_projections_are_not_checkout_artifacts }

fn demand_engine_program_body() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  [
    quote(blocks: [
      p(text: "**Goal:** ordinary sequential .dag runs at its loosest lawful dependency structure, with no authored dependency rows, widths or private scheduler. **Method:** one semantic demand authority, deterministic minimization, lawful decomposition and separately selected realization/materialization. **Validation:** the milestone controls, complete result/refusal contracts and deletion censuses below. This carrier remains the design authority; prose is not implementation. M0 establishes authority additively. Every later root replacement deletes its predecessor with the named production consumer. Decision status and triggers are retained in §7, including D14's ensure authority, M2V (2026-10-22) and D16's five rulings (2026-10-25); a ruling does not discharge its checker trigger. [Original deliberations and receipts](https://github.com/gunb-ai/gunbc/blob/06d3c1c3149a7420c4a548b9aba9b15c721c635d/dag/gunbc/plans/demand_engine_program.dag)."),
      p(text: "**Operator direction carried (2026-09-14/15):** concurrency comes through dependency structure, demand-driven like Bazel's execution phase; one shared ready queue drained by leased capacity -- no widths, waves, batches or shard plans; every existing width/concurrency mechanism is deleted, not frozen or kept behind a serial fallback; future programs inherit this without knowing DependencyView or DependencyRelation; realization is how things happen and materialization is how things are presented/stored; key definitions and hashing are a conformance concern; local processes are fabric supply bounded by an observed offer (in CI, the runner's caps); remote execution is a later strategy over the same graph and keys; no half migrations."),
      p(text: "**Third-party review folded in (side chat, 2026-09-15):** the universal unit is a semantic demand over a node and its context, not a raw node; demand identity and evaluation identity are two keys, and result content identity is a third joined by EvaluationResultBinding; semantic result contracts are not derived from cost; algebraic laws must be established under an observation contract before they license decomposition; occurrence identity is genuine identity at occurrence grain; dynamic dependencies across effects need a chosen protocol; the order below names its first authority cut. Section 7 records D1-D14 as ruled or deferred-with-trigger."),
      p(text: "**D1, ruled 2026-09-15 and amended 2026-10-18:** eval is the semantic judgment. Demand derivation/minimization are deterministic authorities; alternative handlers must be extensionally equivalent. Policy selects admissible realization/materialization handlers, never a different semantic population. A realization may refine internal work, regroup under applicable law and choose order/placement/transport. Emitted Rust is the selected first production realization; the interpreter remains production until that route implements the complete judgment/refusal contract, then becomes an offline differential oracle. The engine consumes generic prerequisites and share/admission verdicts, never effect payloads. Compiler-derived templates, relation constructors, conditional alternatives and membership slots govern runtime instantiation: Branch/Match/Loop use runtime selectors; host-call results are ordinary opaque values; external collection members instantiate only declared templates. Runtime invents no dependency kind. Decomposition alone consumes typed law evidence at its honest rung and carried bound."),
    ]),
    h2(text: "1. Goal and displaced cost"),
    p(text: "**Goal.** An author writes ordinary, sequential-looking `.dag`. The compiler derives the semantic demands the program makes and their dependencies from program structure, effect contracts, external-input observations and established algebraic laws. A demand engine evaluates only what requested targets transitively need, releases dependents the moment a required result is available, produces each equal share-admitted demand once, serves repeated demands from a store, and places heavy work on leased local processes (later, remote executors). None of this is authored per feature."),
    p(text: "**First full-corpus reading (operator run, 2026-09-15, main `1d5c804fe6a`, instrument: the native fold's `[native-cost-partition]` line, verdict NativeDriverCostReconciled).** The bounded-slice framing this document inherited -- per-module prepare versus eval -- was pre-registered against the full fold and FALSIFIED: on the full corpus prepare and eval together are a small fraction of the driver span. What dominates is `context`, the assemble-once corpus ingest (the receipt's scanned_paths and ingested_paths rows against the count of identities actually evaluated); `receipt_admission` is the next row, orders of magnitude above the evaluation it admits; and the emit-plus-build of the native compiler, whose closure fingerprint was identical to the previous run's, sits OUTSIDE the partition as a large share of the process wall. The shares and counts live in that run's receipt and persisted rows; none is restated here. What follows from it is separated into findings and ruled work (side-chat ruling, 2026-09-15). RULED (amended 2026-10-02, operator-directed build-materialization program): the first LIVE cross-run provider consumer is `RepositoryBuildArtifactRequest` -- the repository's release binaries, built once per landing in the merge queue, promoted by one canonical publisher and served to hw boot and fleet through the fabric blob plane (`std.fabric_blob`, docs/plans/fabric-storage.md step 4A) -- and it establishes the blob-backed store spine. The ruling below is unchanged in substance and becomes the first NATIVE SELF-HOST GENERATION family on that same spine: both families use one provider and store contract, and neither redefines keying, retention or realization. The first native self-host generation family for M1.c is `NativeCompilerArtifactRequest` (an IdempotentEffect after M1's contract) consuming the COMPLETE `PreMaterializationIdentity` -- producer compiler, source closure, target model, toolchain, build configuration including the `RustCratePartitionIdentity` of D9, required lens contract -- with the lookup placed BEFORE the work it avoids: a hit on a closure identity derivable from the canonical `.dag` closure bypasses both emission and the cargo build, and the receipt states which boundary it bypassed rather than claiming the whole outer span; discriminator: same complete identity -> verified hit, emitted-source writer not entered, cargo not entered; changed producer/toolchain/partition/lens contract -> miss, emit and build. NEXT DERIVATION, not yet ruled: context assembly is the first engine-cut candidate -- the engine needs an authority cheaper than full ingestion (a source-root manifest, per-file parse or module-summary judgments, the module dependency closure of the requested targets) so that undemanded modules are not parsed, normalized, resolved or loaded, which is a vertical stage cut of M6's kind moved forward into M3a; enumeration may remain. Context identity is NOT tree identity alone: it depends on the requested target population, the sealed read set, tree identity, compiler and evaluator semantics identity, grammar identity, ingestion policy, external observations and the occurrence contract, and the recommended reuse shape is occurrence-neutral per-file/module artifacts from which a graph-local context is assembled -- a separate materialization cut, never hidden inside the engine cut because both attack one timing row. M3a is amended only after the exact context producer, child judgment grain, displaced loop and bypass discriminator are named. REQUIRED CHAIN READ: `receipt_admission` -- subpartition it (row decoding, identity joins, population reconciliation, canonicalization, completeness checks, cost reconciliation, rendering), pre-register which producer multiplicities should collapse, and move a repeated full-population join to a qualified carrier only on that reading; no receipt cache and no weakened admission from the single figure. REQUIRED PRE-ANCHOR CENSUS: every refusal attributed and contract-classified at identity grain (subject, requested judgment, v2 outcome, attributed cause, contract disposition, v1 outcome as differential evidence only, owner, remedy or declared capability boundary), with the terminal populations named -- accepted where the contract requires refusal; refused where the contract requires acceptance; typed refusal permitted by the contract; unattributed refusal; result differing from the complete result contract -- the unattributed population reaching zero before any anchor (D10). Re-derive the numbers from that run's persisted rows and partition line, not from this paragraph."),
    h2(text: "2. The model (terminal architecture)"),
    h3(text: "2.1 Semantics, demand, minimization, materialization, realization, policy"),
    p(text: "DESIGN section 3 keeps interface, realization and policy as three facts, and section 2 minimizes the demand graph before materializing answers. The run of a program is therefore composed, not identified with its execution strategy:"),
    TableBlock {
      header: row(cells: [cell(text: "Layer"), cell(text: "Owns"), cell(text: "Never owns")]),
      alignments: [AlignNone, AlignNone, AlignNone],
      rows: [
        row(cells: [cell(text: "Semantics (eval)"), cell(text: "what judgment a program denotes, sequential as authored"), cell(text: "order, concurrency, placement, storage")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "Demand derivation"), cell(text: "deterministic authority: which judgments requested targets require"), cell(text: "how or where they are computed; policy selection")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "Demand minimization"), cell(text: "deterministic authority: one production serving several demands at their least common ancestor"), cell(text: "cost-based choices; policy selection")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "Materialization"), cell(text: "interface with admissible handlers: recompute, share, memoize, encoding, retention, store"), cell(text: "what outputs a judgment owes; the demand population")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "Realization"), cell(text: "interface with admissible handlers: order, concurrency, grouping, placement, transport"), cell(text: "semantic identity; the demand population")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "Policy"), cell(text: "selects ONLY among admissible materialization and realization handlers (admissibility, trust, resource fit, retention, cost ranking)"), cell(text: "semantics, derivation, minimization, or output obligations")]),
      ],
    },
    p(text: "In the corpus's existing wording (`v2.std.materialize`): `run ≜ realize ∘ materialize ∘ dependency_view`, with minimization before materialize and eval supplying each demanded judgment's meaning. The operator's framing -- realization is how things happen, materialization is how things are presented and stored -- is exactly the two right-hand layers; eval names the semantic judgment (D1, ruled 2026-09-15)."),
    h3(text: "2.2 The unit: a semantic demand, not a raw node"),
    p(text: "`Node` is the universal structural carrier; not every node is independently runnable, and one structural node answers differently under different environments, inferred facts, resolved bindings, interpretations, external observations and semantics versions. The unit of demand is a **judgment** over structure and context, keyed through the existing node structure without minting a second graph. `JudgmentContract` -- subject/input schema, result contract, demand nature, identity derivation, persistence projection -- is THE authority; `std.materialization_provider` `ArtifactRequest` is its persistence projection. A hand-maintained universal `JudgmentKind` coproduct beside `ArtifactRequest` is refused; a closed coproduct is admissible only as a derived projection of the contract population. Semantic demand grain, realization dispatch grain and materialization storage grain are separate facts: thousands of tiny demands may run in one in-process continuation, be fused into one child-process request, or be persisted as one aggregate artifact."),
    p(text: "**Fine authority; selectable execution grain (operator direction 2026-10-21).** Any demand may be ordered individually; no demand must be dispatched individually. Every judgment over a node and its context may be an endpoint of a prerequisite or activation relation, and no realization imposes a minimum semantic grain. Tracking, dispatch, execution partition, fusion, batching and placement are realization facts; storage aggregation, retention, cache versus recompute and persisted encoding are materialization facts (D1). Both are selected under the shared decision authority, and one decision may weigh candidates spanning both interfaces while its receipt still names the realization and the materialization handler it selected. A realization is a refinement, not a quotient: it derives an internal work graph from the semantic judgments and partitions that work into execution units, so it may decompose one judgment (tiling), group several (fusion of a dependent region, batching of compatible independent instances), repartition across communicating stages (pipeline formation), replicate admissible pure internal work, or keep a unit one-to-one. The correspondence from units and their results to the semantic obligations they discharge is many-to-many and checked, and the fine judgment graph stays authoritative. The refinement is admissible only when it preserves every cross-unit prerequisite and every internal prerequisite; activation, so an untaken alternative is still judged at compile time but never becomes a runtime demand; every effect and access obligation, never choosing an order for an unresolved conflict; identity boundaries; the complete result-and-refusal contract; and well-foundedness of the unit graph, which is checked rather than inherited. Production multiplicity follows the judgment's demand nature and operation contract: equal share-admitted semantic demands attach to one admitted production; a realization may replicate or recompute pure internal work where the result binding stays sound (rematerialization into two units to avoid a cross-unit edge), but may neither collapse distinct fresh-effect occurrences nor duplicate one effect occurrence; further effect attempts are admitted only under the operation's idempotency, retry or uncertain-completion contract, and every attempt stays represented in execution evidence."),
    p(text: "**Refinement, not per-node bookkeeping.** A coarse unit carries a static refinement mapping, bound to its realization identity, naming the judgments it discharges. It may be represented symbolically (a template, a context and an instance domain) rather than enumerated when that representation is complete, exact and decidably expandable; enumeration stays admissible, and is required where a consumer must join or report individual obligations (which was absent, which duplicated). A digest binds the representation and never substitutes for the material that join needs. The runtime receipt is per execution unit: realization and refinement identities, actual activation, completion evidence and result content identity. A separately admitted finer realization of the same region may be demanded for qualification, diagnosis or a disputed result; it is not overhead on every production run. The open A4 cut is the first fine-grained execution precedent: its per-occurrence receipts and root reconciliation against eval give one side of the future correspondence. The first coarse-unit consumer must bind a unit-level receipt to an admitted refinement and discharge the same obligations without per-node production receipts."),
    p(text: "**Schedules over templates.** Ordering and grain rules are stated once per demand template and apply to every instance without enumerating them. A template-level legality result holds only within the applicability domain its evidence carries; instance-specific subjects, aliases, dimensions, branch selections, observations and resource facts are checked when the instance is admitted unless a prior proof quantifies over them. Precedent: the polyhedral model gives every loop instance in an iteration domain a logical timestamp through a schedule function and treats tiling, fusion and parallelization as transformations of that function; Halide (Ragan-Kelley et al., PLDI 2013) separates the algorithm from a schedule that may tile, fuse or vectorize without changing the result. The difference here is that the schedule is selected through `std.decision` under M4's measured costs rather than authored."),
    p(text: "**Grain selection is economics, not semantics.** Choosing an execution partition is a realization decision priced under M4 through `std.decision` `select_realization`, never a change to which judgments the program demands; the objective is total cost, which is sometimes fine-grained. The correctness boundaries a unit may not cross (an unresolved dependency disposition, an activation choice the handler cannot preserve, an effect or access conflict, a placement, trust, provider or isolation change, an externally observable completion boundary, a refusal whose canonical behavior cannot be preserved) are admissibility constraints and are not priced away; early availability to an outside consumer, reuse value and failure attribution are economic axes, not boundaries. A coarsened region is not an opaque boundary: an opaque host call's declared boundary is the finest structure presently modeled, a modeling frontier, while a coarsened region keeps its fine authority beneath it and may be re-realized at finer grain. Neither licenses a realization to discard detail the model already carries. Uninstantiated (no consumer has demanded the instance), blocked (instantiated, a prerequisite unavailable) and unresolved (no unique lawful producer) are three states, never one."),
    h3(text: "2.3 Identity: named keying relations, with evaluation split from result content"),
    p(text: "Following `docs/plans/keying-relation-design.md` (a key is a named relation within a named scope):"),
    TableBlock {
      header: row(cells: [cell(text: "Identity"), cell(text: "Denotes"), cell(text: "Scope"), cell(text: "Derived from"), cell(text: "Existing authority")]),
      alignments: [AlignNone, AlignNone, AlignNone, AlignNone, AlignNone],
      rows: [
        row(cells: [cell(text: "structural node identity"), cell(text: "what a node is"), cell(text: "global"), cell(text: "Merkle fold of kind, edge labels, child identities; occurrence excluded"), cell(text: "`v2.std.node` `content_hash`")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "demand identity"), cell(text: "the question asked"), cell(text: "global"), cell(text: "judgment kind + structural subject + explicit arguments + static semantic context"), cell(text: "none general yet; `std.materialization_provider` `request_key` for today's families")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "dependency read set"), cell(text: "the exact judgments and observations an answer consumed"), cell(text: "one answer"), cell(text: "sealed when the demand reaches its terminal value"), cell(text: "none yet")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "evaluation identity"), cell(text: "the judgment under its exact consumed inputs"), cell(text: "global, location- and provider-free"), cell(text: "demand identity + sealed read-set identities + observation identities + evaluator/compiler semantics identity"), cell(text: "`std.materialization_provider` `request_key` / `artifact_request_key` (the input-derived key; analogue)")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "result content identity"), cell(text: "digest of what that evaluation produced"), cell(text: "global, content of the result"), cell(text: "canonical encoding of the produced outputs"), cell(text: "`std.materialization_provider` output-part digests (analogue)")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "occurrence identity"), cell(text: "this authored occurrence"), cell(text: "one allocator (one source graph)"), cell(text: "the graph-scoped allocator; never content, span, spelling or path"), cell(text: "`std.occurrence_identity` scope law; `ScopedOccurrenceRef` for cross-graph references")]),
      ],
    },
    p(text: "**EvaluationResultBinding** joins an evaluation identity to the result content identity it produced. Same evaluation identity with different content identities is a conflict/impurity, never convergence. Do not call the input-derived key \"this answer\"; that nickname mixed the judgment with its product."),
    p(text: "**Keys are algorithmic.** Each identity is a total derivation into a canonical encoding that is injective over its domain. A finite hash of that encoding is never injective. The hash family is chosen by sharing scope, and each scope declares a collision disposition. Structural FNV (`Fnv1a64Structural`) is a bucket/index only, never silent equality: the exact canonical preimage is compared before serving. A cross-run or store key uses an approved cryptographic family appropriate to the named relation and scope; a hit still verifies the canonical preimage before serving. Upstream-mandated SHA-1 (git) is admitted only as THAT upstream relation's identity with its explicit collision disposition -- it is not a general cross-run family. Live migration named here: `std.materialization_provider` `request_key` is FNV-structured while its requests already cross persistent-store boundaries; M1 migrates that route to an admitted cryptographic family, or records an explicit preimage-verified exception for that named relation until the migration lands. Hand-authored, positional or lossy encodings are defects; C1 is the recorded specimen -- a live cross-parse memo identity whose digest used the wrong unary byte domain. The `conformance-identity` domain owns the key RELATION (what relation, what scope, what complete canonical preimage derives it, what equality and collision disposition govern it); identity owns whether the declared inputs are complete. `conformance-realization` owns whether a handler, provider or placement faithfully realizes an admitted identity and result contract (completeness, readback, retention, transport, refusal, binding obligations) -- whether the handler consumes the admitted key without substituting, truncating or bypassing it. Cache purity is one discriminator across that boundary, not the whole realization domain."),
    p(text: "**Occurrence identity is not a computation key and is not demoted.** It distinguishes structurally equal occurrences (the two `x` in `x + x`) for binding, diagnostics and provenance. A result that depends only on occurrence-neutral structure is already reusable across graphs; a result containing or indexed by occurrence identities must declare one contract: semantic-only (persist occurrence-neutral values, rebind locally after load), scoped (persist `ScopedOccurrenceRef`s usable while their scope is addressable), or rebinding (persist structural anchors plus a proved mapping into the destination graph). A bare occurrence ordinal is never interpreted in another allocator."),
    h3(text: "2.4 Dependencies are derived; declarations of underivable facts stay"),
    p(text: "The engine's relation is `v2.std.dependency` `DependencyRelation` over demand identity; compiler `DependencyView`s project into it. Semantic dataflow and control flow come from the section 4 behaviors: a call demands callee and arguments; a use of a `Bind`-bound name demands that binding's value, and nothing else in the `Bind`'s body waits on it -- the body is lexical scope, not a prerequisite, and textual position establishes no dependency (operator, 2026-10-18). A bare block that only limits which names are visible is the same scope and adds no relation. A use waits on the value's availability, never on the enclosing `Bind`'s completion, so no use cycles through its own binder; `Branch` and `Match` demand condition or scrutinee, then exactly one arm of a statically known set, chosen by that value -- a static demand with a runtime selector, never a dynamic edge; products demand components per construction; `Loop` demands its static body with a data-valued trip count under its descent evidence. A host call -- a shell invocation, a REST request -- is a demand at its call site and nothing more: its inputs are demanded, its result is an ordinary value, and the engine neither interprets nor expands what the call does inside. A list that arrives from such a call is consumed like any authored list: `map`, `fold` and per-item `Match` over it are static demands whose membership is data. Structural containment (`DependencyView` `Contains`) stays a separate structural fact for its existing readers -- `v2.lens.wiring_liveness` reads it from an output toward its declared inputs -- and demand relations, producer result to consuming demand, are derived beside it, never by re-orienting it. The compiler therefore derives every demand template, relation constructor, conditional alternative and typed membership slot; runtime never invents a new dependency kind or an untyped relation. Selector values activate statically declared alternatives; runtime collection members and call arguments instantiate concrete demands and relations through their declared templates, and every instantiated subject is keyed, admitted, entered into the read set and scheduled by the one demand engine -- a realization handler that hid those child demands internally would be a second engine. Data edges carry operand bindings -- which producer output satisfies which consumer input. **Hand-authored rows that restate derivable dataflow or control flow are deleted** (today: `v2.program` `program_run_dependencies`). **Declarations of facts the language cannot derive stay**: effect declarations, host-call input/output contracts, idempotency and freshness contracts, external observation identities, explicit workflow barriers, resource and trust constraints, opaque implementation boundaries. The engine never interprets them. EFFECTS ARE ORDERED BY ACCESS, NEVER BY POSITION (operator, 2026-10-18; its five parts ruled 2026-10-25, section 7.1). Dependencies come from three sources only: the data flow of values, the selected alternative of a `Branch` or `Match`, and the data flow of access to live resources. An effect operation consumes checked access to each resource it touches and produces the continuation available after its declared outcome. That access is the compiler's own bookkeeping over D13's derived dependency demand and its subjects, never an authored handle or parameter: for a transparent body the author neither declares nor passes it, because a function that uses a resource should not have to say so a second time (D13). What the author writes to order two effects is ordinary program structure -- the second uses the first's result, or sits in the alternative the first's outcome selects -- and the compiler derives the access flow from that. Access cannot be duplicated: it is exclusive, or shared inside a checked read phase that must close before exclusive use resumes, and the check reaches through closures, records, helpers and loops. Read-before-write and write-before-read are therefore different data: the second operation consumes the continuation the first produced. Two operations holding one predecessor conflict; a conflicting pair with no derived relation refuses, typed and located, and neither the engine nor a realization chooses a direction. An order between unrelated resources (a log line, then an exit) is the first operation's completion evidence consumed by the second -- data again, never a second mechanism. Four facts stay distinct even where one handle carries several: authorization (`std.effect_grant`), access discipline, completion evidence, and retained snapshot identity; a version number, content hash or lease epoch preserves no bytes, so a read of old contents after a replacement is admitted only under a snapshot contract that retains them. A failed or uncertain effect yields the continuation its outcome contract states, never the predecessor as though nothing happened, and a refusal after an effect is not a rollback. Derived orders are projected into the generic prerequisite relations `v2.std.dependency` already carries (`EffectDependsOn`, `BarrierBefore`, `PlacementDependsOn`; `ResourceDependsOn` is derived by nothing and read only by a fixture, `v2.test.lens_ownership.resource_dependency`, so the change that first records a derived order either gives it that meaning or deletes it), which the engine consumes exactly like data prerequisites rather than in a private readiness graph in some handler. STATUS: none of this is enforced today -- the interpreter's left-to-right `Bind` traversal is what orders effects now -- and the rule switches on only after the M2 specimens qualify on the v2/native path. Effect analysis also projects into generic share and materialization admissions (`std.materialization_ladder` already separates PureComputation, IdempotentEffect, WorldRead and FreshEffect; a fresh effect is neither attached in flight nor served from a prior result). Realization consumes the full effect and resource envelope to select placement and acquire grants; the host-effect boundary performs the operation (section 2.9; section 2.7 covers placement and grants). An undeclared or unresolved effect never reads as independence: an opaque host call without an established envelope couples conservatively, because not interpreting shell cannot mean treating shell as pure."),
    h3(text: "2.5 The demand engine"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "**Demand closure.** Evaluation starts from requested targets and pulls transitive demands; undemanded judgments never enter lookup, readiness or realization."),
      li(text: "**Static demand schemas, dynamically admitted instances.** A demand's templates and relation constructors are planned before it runs; no dependency kind is discovered at runtime. What runs decides selector values (which arm, how many iterations) and the members of declared collections (which files the shell call listed), each instantiating a concrete demand through a slot the plan already carries with a typed subject shape; the engine keys it, attaches it to any in-flight equal share-admitted demand, detects cycles, inserts its relations and records its read. A running demand whose selected arm or admitted member is not yet available suspends and resumes on availability; that is the whole dynamic protocol (D4). The exact read set -- which arms and members were actually consumed -- is sealed at the terminal value and only then yields the evaluation identity. One equal, share-admitted demand is produced once."),
      li(text: "**Availability, not scheduling.** Per demand: waiting, ready, lease pending, running, suspended, commit pending, available, refused, blocked by a prerequisite. Only available -- the exact result contract admitted -- releases dependents. A verdict bundle full of red tests is available; a crash or a corrupt object is not."),
      li(text: "**One live ready queue.** A demand whose prerequisites are available enters the one queue immediately; any completion immediately re-evaluates its direct dependents. No layers, batches or waves. Discriminating case (A1 -> B and an independent A2): B becomes READY immediately after A1 becomes available, without waiting for A2. Actual simultaneous execution of B with A2 is the first control of the local-child-process cut, not of the one-seat in-process engine."),
      li(text: "**One dispatch policy.** When ready work exceeds capacity: stable order by demand identity, admit the next demand whose requirements obtain a lease, leave unadmittable demands ready. No feature supplies another policy."),
      li(text: "**Typed blocked states.** Cycle, missing edge, refused prerequisite, waiting for capacity, running, suspended, lookup pending, no demand and key conflict are distinct."),
    ]),
    h3(text: "2.6 Lawful algebraic decomposition"),
    p(text: "A dependency relation alone leaves the commonest sequential shape -- a fold whose step reads the previous accumulator -- a chain. Established algebraic laws break chains: associativity with identity licenses regrouping; commutativity, order-free combination; idempotence, collapsing duplicates; a homomorphism, pushing a map through a split; independent product dimensions, concurrent components. Three conditions bound this:"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "**A law is evidence, not a record shape.** Today `Monoid` and `CommutativeMonoid` in `std.algebra` are field-identical: inhabiting them establishes an operation and an identity value, not associativity. Decomposition needs named law evidence such as `AssociativeUnder<ObservationContract>`, `CommutativeUnder`, `IdempotentUnder` and `HomomorphismBetween`, established over the complete observable result -- value, diagnostic population and order, effect requests and order, provenance, refusal shape, termination. An unestablished law leaves the sequential dependence intact."),
      li(text: "**Associativity permits many trees; the canonical one is chosen.** Stable incremental reuse needs an append-stable decomposition (content-defined chunks under a Merkle hierarchy, or a persistent sequence with canonical branching) so that appending invalidates a bounded path by construction."),
      li(text: "**Granularity is priced by total cost**: semantic work plus dependency bookkeeping, queueing, encoding, lookup, lease acquisition, process startup, transfer, commit and reduction -- otherwise splitting wins on paper and loses in execution."),
    ]),
    p(text: "This carries the reduce-spine and idle-lane direction of the since-deleted machine-shape-orthogonal-scheduling plan (computation graph x machine facts x algebraic evidence -> schedule)."),
    h3(text: "2.7 Realization: placement from fabric supply"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "**Placement** gains a truthful local child-process arm; current `std.realization` `Placement` has none, and `LocalInProcess` is not a child process."),
      li(text: "**Supply** is `product.fabric.supply` `SupplierOffer` over a local-process executor: one offer per conserved pool, quantity derived from the observed execution scope (effective cgroup CPU, memory and PID limits and usage) against the measured work footprint and named policy. An unobservable axis refuses; a footprint that does not fit yields zero, never one. In CI the runner slot is the scope."),
      li(text: "**Selection** goes through `product.fabric.selection` `CandidateOffer` and `select_supply`, with capacity-class admission, even with one offer. Selection is not occupancy."),
      li(text: "**Occupancy is two-level**: a host-global grant reserves each realizing run's scope (in CI the runner slot already is that grant); an in-parent atomic ledger subdivides it per demand by resource vector. No committed lease, no spawn; release on every terminal arm, only after quiescence. A `SupplierOffer` with no committed lease path is half-wired and is not an occupancy result."),
      li(text: "**Nested schedulers** (cargo, make) take tokens from the demand's committed claim and never choose their own width."),
      li(text: "**The crate structure of the emitted closure is a Rust realization over a derived compilation-unit interface, and it is a third lever beside scheduling and reuse.** The native route emits `src/v2/compiler/compile.dag`'s closure as ONE crate (`gunbc-emitted-closure-src-v2-compiler-00-compile-dag`, lib `v1_compiled`; the module count, line count and dependency list are read from that crate's manifest and the native route's emit log, not from here). rustc's front half is single-threaded per crate, so on a many-core builder that crate's dominant phase runs on one core; cargo parallelizes across crates, and its crate-level scheduling is an existing realization handler doing what it already does -- so a crate DAG is emitted STRUCTURE, not an engine width, and lies outside the no-widths ruling. The homes already exist and this program reuses them, never a hand-authored crate roster (a second positional authority for the module graph): `v2.std.compilers.compilation_unit` owns separate-compilation units, their derived interfaces (referenced declarations and by-value type-definition closure) and unit dependencies; `v2.workflow.rust_crate_partition` owns Rust partition candidates, Rust admissibility (`validate_partition_r4`: `SccSplit`, `UnitGraphCycle`, `ByValueTypeCycle`) and the balanced policy resolver (`PolicyPartitionParam` -> `policy_partition_assignment`, admitted through `resolve_partition_outcome`) beside stage0's explicit interim partition; `v2.workflow.emitted_closure_crate_partition` derives the emitted closure's module DAG from the resolved dependency facts and resolves it under that policy, with no emitted-route consumer until the crate rendering lands; `std.decision` selects within an admitted, funded candidate field; `v1.emit_rust` and the native route RENDER the selected workspace -- `pub use`, external crate paths, manifests, a stable facade crate -- and never decide it. Source acyclicity (DESIGN §3) is necessary, not sufficient: candidates derive from the typed resolved-reference graph plus runtime and emitter edges, complete cross-unit interfaces and Rust admissibility. The textual `crate::` graph over emitted `.rs` is an INSTRUMENT, not an authority: its first receipt exposed an emitted-reference SCC involving `std_content_hash`, `std_occurrence_identity` and `v2_std_node` (the receipt owns the population and membership); that specimen is not a source-import cycle -- the authored imports are acyclic -- so that SCC is an unresolved emitted-reference SCC to be classified by edge provenance (semantic source reference | derived cross-unit interface | runtime/prelude | reexport/facade | emitter-generated helper | lexical false positive) before it may enter the partition authority as an atom; an emitter-added reverse edge is an emitter defect, a genuine Rust-target requirement is a realization edge. `v1_rt` (referenced by nearly every emitted module, and already alone in the stage0 `v1-stage0-runtime` crate) is a foundation-crate CANDIDATE and the implementation of a runtime-support contract; the obligation that survives self-hosting is that the emitted closure receives a runtime interface faithfully realizing every intrinsic its modules use, not that a module of that name exists. The native consumer keeps one stable root/facade surface; internal crate names are realization details. The selected partition enters generation identity as a canonical `RustCratePartitionIdentity` -- the module-to-unit assignment, the derived unit interfaces and dependencies, and the stable facade identity -- named as a component of `build_configuration` in `GenerationIdentity` / `PreMaterializationIdentity`, so two partitions of one closure are different build inputs, never same-evaluation/different-content impurity; the `SelectionReceipt` travels beside it as decision provenance and is not itself a build input, because two decisions under different evidence or policy may select the same partition."),
    ]),
    h3(text: "2.8 Materialization: share, recompute, memoize, store"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "**Share** within a run is demand minimization: one equal, share-admitted demand is produced once, and later requesters attach in flight. Equal pure demands share one production; fresh-effect invocations are distinct productions; world reads share only under an admitted observation/staleness identity; idempotent effects share or retry only under their declared idempotency contract."),
      li(text: "**Recompute** is an explicit decision below the materialization ladder's cost floor (`std.materialization_ladder`), not a default. Today the floor is an exempt roster; M4 replaces it with a measured serve-versus-recompute decision."),
      li(text: "**Memoize and store** under collision-resistant evaluation identity bound to a verified result content identity (`extdeps.realization.materialization_store_local` for inline structured results on one host; the fabric blob plane, `std.fabric_blob`, for packed results across hosts -- a persisted output is not necessarily inline text or a local file). Lookup precedes capacity: a verified hit takes no lease and releases dependents; an unavailable or corrupt store refuses and never reads as a miss. A hit still verifies the canonical preimage; same evaluation identity with a different content identity refuses as impurity."),
      li(text: "**Cross-run hit protocol.** Two routes are admissible. Manifest-validation (first consumer): DemandIdentity looks up a MUTABLE evaluation-index record \{read-set manifest, EvaluationIdentity, ResultContentIdentity\}, validates that current member identities match the sealed read set, then serves the IMMUTABLE content-addressed result or takes a typed arm. Four arms with different owners: index absent is ordinary cold state; index stale is a validation miss -- re-evaluate and CAS a successor generation; index corrupt or unreadable is typed `EvaluationIndexCorrupt` / `EvaluationIndexUnreadable` with an observation/repair receipt and a named quarantine-delete-rebuild transition -- re-evaluation may FOLLOW that typed transition and is never the first and only observable outcome; immutable result absent is a materialization miss. The index is a mutable alias with compare-and-set/generation on update: two evaluations racing for the same DemandIdentity CAS the index; the loser re-reads and either attaches to the winner's verified binding or re-evaluates if the winner aborted. Same EvaluationIdentity with different ResultContentIdentity is a conflict, never a CAS winner. The immutable store stays content-addressed independently of the mutable alias. Discovery-first: re-run pure dependency discovery, form the exact read set, derive EvaluationIdentity, then immutable lookup. **Ruled for the first consumer: manifest-validation.** Discovery-first re-executes the dependency pass the demand engine already performs and remains an admissible later handler."),
      li(text: "**Semantic result contracts are interface, not cost.** `JudgmentContract` is the authority; `ArtifactRequest` is only its persistence projection, derived when persistence is selected -- contract first, then optional materialization, never cost first."),
      li(text: "**Encoding.** Results crossing a process or run boundary use a canonical, versioned encoding with decode refusal and identity readback; shared context is materialized results consumed by dependents, never an inherited process address space."),
    ]),
    h3(text: "2.9 Host-effect realization of the emitted closure (the gate on leaving the interpreter)"),
    p(text: "D10 anchors production on the native (emitted) route with no interpreter fallback. That is not reachable while the emitted closure cannot perform an effect, and the receipt of gunbc#11474 -- an OPEN pull request proposing a v2-exclusive CLI (its `src/v2/cli/compile_cli.dag` and `NativeCliDriver` are not on main and are cited here as that PR's receipt, not as tree symbols) -- established the current state by execution, not inference: a resource operation on an ordinary `fn` is a located compiler refusal, because a resource METHOD is admitted only where the function declares that resource through its `uses` clause (the mechanism sentence first written here -- that resource operations resolve only inside workflow functions realized by the seed interpreter -- is FALSE and is superseded by D12 below, which deletes the workflow-function concept outright; the refusal it was offered to explain is real and unchanged); `v1.compiler.emit_rust` `emit_resource_def` renders a resource as an `async_trait` interface with NO implementor; and no emitted closure on disk -- the self-host closure or the CLI closure -- has ever carried a resource trait or a function taking a resource parameter. The RESOURCE path in the emitted target -- `std.resources` interfaces and their implementors -- is unexercised end to end, interface emission included. The filesystem SERVICE path is not: `extdeps.filesystem.filesystem_io` `Filesystem` already has its one Rust realization handler bound to that shape, `v1.compiler.emit_rust` `emit_file_call` (path template, verb, typed read/write result fields, empty-path guard, io-error kind), which `v1.compiler.emit` names as the one realization handler; what is missing is not a handler but its binding to an ordinary-function entry under D11. The PRODUCTION host boundary today is one hand-authored function: `v1.compiler.emit_rust` `emit_source_root_eval_driver_main_rs`, the rendered main that reads argv, walks and reads the source roots, reads one monotonic `Instant` for the cost partition, reads peak resident bytes from `/proc/self/status`, writes both output sinks and exits -- and its dissolution is already owned by the seed-growth row `gunbc.source_root_eval_driver_seed_growth` `source_root_eval_driver_seed_growth_justification`, whose trigger names exactly this program's terminal shape (a `.dag` driver receiving those host operations AS VALUES so the rendered main becomes one call into that fold) and says a partial migration does not retire the row. That function is the SUBJECT of E2 and E4 below, and of M3a's deletion of its scheduling loop: one function, one dissolution trigger, three cuts that each delete part of it and none of which retires the row alone. gunbc#11474's own rendered main, which reads argv and files, writes output and exits directly, is a second instance of the same shape and an OBSERVED INTERIM SHIM, not the terminal template: direct `std::env`, `std::fs`, `print!` and `/proc` calls are themselves native realizations, and a second one beside a bound handler is the parallel-authority fork §3 forbids."),
    p(text: "**The terminal shape (side-chat ruling, 2026-09-16; §3 interface / realization / policy).** The `.dag` program owns the effect REQUEST, its semantic result-and-refusal contract, and every decision that follows from the returned value. A concrete native HANDLER owns the OS interaction and returns modeled values or receipts. Entry POLICY admits and binds the handler. The generated `main` is the composition root and nothing more: construct and admit the selected handlers, bind them to the entry, pass argv and input boundary values, invoke the `.dag` entry, realize `std.process` `ProcessExit` -- the precedent, because it is the program's declared exit intent realized at the boundary. Every effect cut below DELETES the corresponding direct per-driver host logic it displaces; a handler landing beside the old implementation is not a cut. The homes are the ones the corpus already has, and no read/write vocabulary is minted beside them: `std.resources` `Filesystem` is the semantic resource interface and `extdeps.filesystem.filesystem_io` `Filesystem` the concrete operation interface with typed read/write outcomes that the native Filesystem handler realizes. The driver's two measurements already have carriers and are NOT new effects: monotonic elapsed is `std.realization_measurement` `ObserveElapsedAtSubject`, realized at the seam `observed_monotonic_nanos`, and peak resident bytes is `ObservePeakResidentAtSubject`, realized by `observed_peak_resident_bytes`, whose executing transport is `extdeps.posix.rusage` (`getrusage(RUSAGE_SELF).ru_maxrss` -- the portable interface, modeled precisely because the repository had been reading `/proc/self/status` VmHWM, which exists on one kernel family). The driver's own `proc_status_kib(\"VmHWM\")` / `proc_status_kib(\"VmRSS\")` read is the leftover second transport of that one operation -- transport fused per driver, §3's exact tell -- and E4 deletes it in favour of the rusage-backed seam; `/proc` is not the seam and a cut that treats it as one re-fuses the fork. The driver's `rss_bytes` reading has no `Observe*` carrier at all: E4 either names its carrier beside `ObservePeakResidentAtSubject` (a current-resident observation is a different question from peak) or deletes the reading if no consumer demands it. The driver reads no wall clock (its `SystemTime` path was deleted so the partition compares readings from one source) and no CPU time; `std.resources` `Clock` and `extdeps.clock` `Clock` (a calendar `Timestamp` service) are different contracts from that `Instant` span and are not this boundary's subject. Only argv and the two output sinks have no modeled carrier today; each names its precise missing carrier in its cut rather than a placeholder."),
    p(text: "The gap splits in two, and this program admits the minimum of it. GAP A -- effects modeled but with no emitted realization: `Filesystem`, `Clock`, `Network`, `Entropy`, `AuthContext` each emit a trait with no implementor and no binding of a handler to an entry. GAP B -- host facts read inline by the seed's rendered `main` instead of through a bound realization: argv and stdout/stderr (no modeled carrier), and the elapsed and peak-resident observations that `[native-cost-partition]` needs (carriers exist in `std.realization_measurement`; the binding does not). Ruled scope (operator, 2026-09-16): the native route needs FILESYSTEM plus the Gap-B host facts; `Clock`, `Network`, `Entropy` and `AuthContext` are NOT realized in the emitted closure by this program -- `std.resources` `Clock` (calendar time, a live effect elsewhere in the corpus) has no native handler cut here and is disposed exactly like the other three, not left in an unstated middle between the Instant seam and the refused set. That is a scope ruling, not a rung drop -- no native route ever held them -- on four conditions that make it one: D10's required denominator explicitly excludes judgments requiring them; admitting such a judgment to the native route produces a TYPED native-realization refusal; the absence of a Rust implementor is never the refusal mechanism (depending on how the closure is instantiated it becomes a rustc error, a missing linkage or a dead interface -- evidence of the gap, not a refusal); and no interpreter fallback executes the excluded judgment. The typed carrier is total: `NativeEffectRealizationAdmission = NativeEffectHandlerBound { effect_contract, handler_identity } | NativeEffectOutsideProductionDenominator { effect_contract, denominator } | NativeEffectRealizationUnavailable { effect_contract, cause }`, and D10 consumes that verdict for every effect reachable from its required denominator."),
    p(text: "Sequence, as standalone realization cuts owned by the CLI lane and not blocking M1.b/M1.c. D11 is RULED FIRST (below), because an ordinary function cannot call a resource today, so no probe can qualify the ordinary-function route before the language decision is taken, and no entry can be bound without knowing which entry form owns the requirement. **E0** the CLI closure builds -- its first entry pulled `v2.extdeps.languages.rust` into a closure the self-host route never compiled, exposing rustc errors in never-before-emitted modules (the dominant class is one emitter defect, a missing `'static` on emitted generic parameters); errors are grouped by root cause and fixed in the emitter, never per file; instrument: the emitted CLI closure compiles. **E1** a probe before a realization: one minimal `.dag` module declaring a resource and one ORDINARY function carrying the explicit effect requirement of D11, emitted and built with no concrete host implementation; instrument: the resource interface and the call-site signature emit and compile, and breaking either the interface or the call-site lowering reds. E1 falsifies whether the emitter implements D11; it does not choose D11. **E2** binding the EXISTING realization of `extdeps.filesystem.filesystem_io` -- `v1.compiler.emit_rust` `emit_file_call`, the one handler bound to that shape -- to the ordinary-function entry under D11's explicit requirement, and deleting the source-root walk and inline `std::fs` reads from `emit_source_root_eval_driver_main_rs`; E2 authors no second filesystem transport, which would be the fork this section names as not a cut (the seed-growth row's subject; E2 deletes only its Filesystem portion and does not retire the row); instrument: the interpreter is bypassed for that operation on the native route and a bypass mutation reds. **E3** total native-effect realization admission -- `NativeEffectRealizationAdmission` computed for every effect in D10's denominator, with the unsupported-effect refusals executing. **E4** the Gap-B boundary migration as values through bound realizations -- argv and stdout/stderr through their newly named carriers, and `ObserveElapsedAtSubject` / `ObservePeakResidentAtSubject` through their existing seams bound at the entry -- each deleting the corresponding direct read from `emit_source_root_eval_driver_main_rs`; instrument as E2. E4 also reconciles the seed-growth row's trigger text, which still names a wall clock the driver no longer reads. E2, E4 and M3a each delete a part of `emit_source_root_eval_driver_main_rs`, but none of them, nor all three together, retires `source_root_eval_driver_seed_growth_justification`: its trigger is a CAPABILITY -- a `.dag` driver function that receives the host operations as values so the rendered main becomes ONE call into that fold, with the phase order, the refusal arms and the terminal marker decided in `.dag` -- and the row itself names the tempting smaller artifacts (a decode alone, a rendered row alone) that do not retire it. The retained orchestration that E2/E4/M3a leave behind is the row's remaining subject; the cut that lands that driver function and reduces `main` to one call is the retiring cut, sequenced after E4 and M3a, and it is named here so no partial cut is mistaken for it. The derisk claims for `emit_compiler_import_closure_from_ingest` that are red under `test/claim/long/` are a separate frontier of the same lane: the claim identities and the discovery/cadence producer that failed to observe them are named in that lane's receipt, and a red that no gate observes is silence, filed as such."),
    h2(text: "3. Relationship to existing authorities and plans"),
    TableBlock {
      header: row(cells: [cell(text: "Authority / plan"), cell(text: "Relationship")]),
      alignments: [AlignNone, AlignNone],
      rows: [
        row(cells: [cell(text: "`v2.std.materialize` (`run ≜ realize ∘ materialize ∘ dependency_view`, analysis-side)"), cell(text: "**Consumed and made live.** Its structural identity grade and spine wording are this program's; M3a is its first runtime.")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "`docs/plans/keying-relation-design.md`"), cell(text: "**Consumed.** Its named-relation-in-scope model is section 2.3; M1 is its first large consumer.")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "realization-measurement-loop plan (since deleted)"), cell(text: "**Consumed.** Its gate (realize(T) content-addressed at minimal placement; uncached non-redundant work is an error) is M5's acceptance.")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "machine-shape-orthogonal-scheduling plan (since deleted)"), cell(text: "**Consumed.** Its reduce-spine, idle-lane and algebraic-evidence direction is section 2.6.")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "resource-aware-scheduler and bounded-input-cost-envelope-scheduling plans (since deleted)"), cell(text: "**Superseded on width.** `spawn_width` formulas are replaced by lease admission against an observed offer. Bounded-input admission and predicted-versus-measured cost roles are retained.")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "`docs/plans/serving-capacity-home-ruling.md`"), cell(text: "**Consistent.** Shared selection, subject-specific occupancy; local processes get their own producer and linearization.")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "content-hash-family-grounding plan (since deleted)"), cell(text: "**Consumed.** This program adds the family-by-sharing-scope rule.")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "`v2.lens.cost`, `std.realization_schedule` `CostAccount`"), cell(text: "**Consumed.** Symbolic cost and measured/predicted axes exist; M4 joins them to demand and realization identities.")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "`v2.workflow.scheduler` / `executor` / `runtime_run`, `module_resolution_plan`; `v2.workflow.realization_runner` scheduling surfaces"), cell(text: "**Split.** M3a cuts the native-fold scheduling loop. M3b migrates `v2.program` and `module_resolution_plan`, relocates `NodeKeyedGraphArtifact`, `NodeKeyedGraphRow`, `NodeKeyedGraphArtifactEntry`, `node_keyed_graph_row_lookup` / `NodeKeyedGraphRowMissing`, `node_keyed_graph_transitive_bytes` / `NodeKeyedGraphTransitiveBytesMissingRow`, and deletes unconsumed scheduler/batch/runner-schedule/`runtime_run` surfaces once the exact producer/consumer/disposition census reaches zero old scheduling consumers. The interpreter is not deleted.")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "`gunbc.source_root_eval_driver_seed_growth` `source_root_eval_driver_seed_growth_justification`"), cell(text: "**Consumed.** The seed-growth row whose subject is the production host boundary `v1.compiler.emit_rust` `emit_source_root_eval_driver_main_rs`; section 2.9's E2/E4 and M3a each delete part of that function toward the row's own trigger, and the row retires by that trigger, never by a partial cut.")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "`std.materialization_provider` `ArtifactRequest`"), cell(text: "**Persistence projection of `JudgmentContract`, not a parallel authority.** Generalized in M2 only as that projection.")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "`conformance-identity` / `conformance-realization`"), cell(text: "**Partition.** `conformance-identity` owns the key RELATION -- what relation, what scope, what complete canonical preimage derives it, what equality and collision disposition govern it -- and whether the declared inputs are complete. `conformance-realization` owns whether a handler, provider or placement faithfully realizes an admitted identity and result contract (completeness, readback, retention, transport, refusal, binding obligations) without substituting, truncating or bypassing the admitted key. Cache purity is one discriminator across that boundary, not the whole realization domain.")]),
      ],
    },
    h2(text: "4. Current census: what each root's deletion breaks"),
    p(text: "**DISCOVERY-ONLY roster of roots**, not a transcribed population. Re-derive dependents by file-mention grep of each root's symbols over production and test trees at the milestone's delete-first branch; do not copy hit counts into this document. Every cut still owes an exact producer/consumer/route/disposition census. The loud census is the fail-closed floor on that delete-first branch, run per milestone before its detailed plan."),
    TableBlock {
      header: row(cells: [cell(text: "Root"), cell(text: "Named production sites (symbols)")]),
      alignments: [AlignNone, AlignNone],
      rows: [
        row(cells: [cell(text: "scheduler layers, executor, realization_runner, runtime_run"), cell(text: "`v2.program`, `module_resolution_plan`, `operand_flow`, a plan row, `doc_graph_roots`, `resolved_graph_cache.rs`")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "hand-authored DependencyView rows"), cell(text: "`v2.program` `program_run_dependencies` and fixture rows")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "v2.lens.parallelism (no independent arm; coupling evidence always unresolved)"), cell(text: "`v2.lens.parallelism`")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "eval as a recursive child fold"), cell(text: "`00_compile` native driver, `v2.program`, `runtime_run`")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "`std.realize_pack` advisory width"), cell(text: "`std.realize_pack`")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "`std.realization_width` fallback / minimum-one"), cell(text: "`std.realization_width`")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "parse sweep unbounded threads"), cell(text: "`claim_executor`, `declaration_index`, `module_path_index`, `namespace_baseline`")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "build parallelism (`ci_compile_jobs`, jobserver, `CARGO_BUILD_JOBS`)"), cell(text: "`ci_compile_jobs`, jobserver, `CARGO_BUILD_JOBS`")]),
        row(cells: [cell(text: "results indexed by occurrence identity crossing graphs"), cell(text: "allocator, minted ids, span index")]),
      ],
    },
    h2(text: "5. Milestones"),
    p(text: "Order: M0 -> M1 -> M2 -> M2V -> M3a -> M3b -> M4 -> M5 -> M6 -> M7. M2V is the first vertical semantic cut (operator, 2026-10-22): every stage on one path reads one derived plan, and it is the first SEMANTIC-BODY scheduling authority cut, at body-judgment grain, for its admitted population. M3a is the first NATIVE-FOLD DRIVER scheduling authority cut (one-seat in-process), at module and declaration grain; remaining scheduler deletion is M3b. Law-bearing decomposition is last: do not optimize stage-wide folds that M6 deletes. Each lists what it makes true, what it deletes in the same change, its production consumer, its qualification and its exit."),
    h3(text: "M0 — Identity is a conformance domain; this program is the authority"),
    p(text: "**Makes true.** `gunbc.design_argument` `conformance_domains` gains `conformance-identity` (keys and hashing); `gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_review_criteria` derives its reviewer; this plan is registered and projected."),
    p(text: "**Deletes in the same change.** Nothing (additive authority)."),
    p(text: "**Production consumer.** The derived review criterion, read on every change."),
    p(text: "**Qualification.** Every home resolves; the reviewer roster and the generated DESIGN.md include the domain (`roadmap_review_role_witness_test` `production_conformance_domains_appear_in_generated_design`, `roadmap_review_function_witness_test` reviewer list)."),
    p(text: "**Exit.** Operator decisions D1-D13 recorded in this document, and side-chat sign-off."),
    h3(text: "M1 — Semantic demand and identity contract"),
    p(text: "M1 lands as three serial cuts, each one PR with one production consumer switched and its displaced route deleted: **M1.a** the JudgmentContract authority with ArtifactRequest as its persistence projection; **M1.b** the keying relations (demand, evaluation, result-content identity, EvaluationResultBinding) for the M1.a families and the request_key store migration; **M1.c** the cross-run hit protocol for one native family (the NativeCompilerArtifactRequest, ruled in section 1), on the blob-backed store spine `RepositoryBuildArtifactRequest` establishes first. D9's crate-partition cut sits between M1.a and M1.b."),
    p(text: "**Makes true.** `JudgmentContract` as the judgment authority (subject/input schema, result contract, demand nature, identity derivation, persistence projection); demand identity, dependency read set, evaluation identity, result content identity and EvaluationResultBinding as named keying relations with injective canonical encodings, family-by-scope hashing and a declared collision disposition per scope; per-judgment result contracts classifying semantic value, diagnostics, provenance, effects and refusal, with each occurrence-bearing output's contract (semantic-only, scoped or rebinding); evaluator and compiler semantics identity; external observation identity. Existing keys (`content_hash`, `request_key`, `typed_module_key`, the parse-table subject, store keys) inhabit the relations. `ArtifactRequest` remains only the persistence projection. `std.materialization_provider` `request_key` migrates off FNV-as-store-key onto an admitted cryptographic family, or is recorded as an explicit preimage-verified exception for that named relation."),
    p(text: "**Deletes in the same change.** A second derivation of the same named relation in the same declared scope; lossy digest arms; any enrolled production key read across a scope its relation does not declare; any hand-maintained universal `JudgmentKind` coproduct standing beside `ArtifactRequest`."),
    p(text: "**Production consumer.** The native fold's existing `ArtifactRequest` families re-expressed as `JudgmentContract` persistence projections, keyed through the relations (no scheduling yet)."),
    p(text: "**Qualification.** Per relation: an injectivity witness over boundary cases of the canonical encoding with a collapsing mutation that reds; a family-by-scope refusal of a structural fingerprint as a cross-run key; a witness that a fingerprint collision is caught by content verification rather than served; a witness that two parses of one program with different occurrence ids yield equal demand and evaluation identities while their occurrence-scoped outputs refuse cross-scope interpretation."),
    p(text: "**Exit.** Every enrolled production key in the native-fold judgment population inhabits a named relation in its declared scope."),
    h3(text: "M2 — Derived dependencies and the dynamic-demand protocol"),
    p(text: "**Makes true.** Static dataflow and control-flow dependencies derived from section 4 behaviors, with operand bindings; Branch/Match/Loop as static demands with runtime selectors; host-call sites as opaque demands whose collection results fill declared membership slots; suspension on an unavailable selector or member, member admission and read-set sealing (D4); `v2.lens.parallelism` answers independence from admitted relations. `ArtifactRequest` stays the persistence projection of `JudgmentContract`."),
    p(text: "**Deletes in the same change.** `v2.program` `program_run_dependencies` and every fixture row that restates derivable dataflow; and `v2.lens.common.algebraic_composition` `parallelism_classifier`'s reading of the structural kinds `Contains` and `BindsTo` as `DataDependent` (a structural view presented to the parallelism lens refuses, located; it establishes neither dependence nor independence). Layer planning over written child order is deleted at M3b."),
    p(text: "**Production consumer.** `v2.program`'s run and `module_resolution_plan`'s module dependencies, derived rather than written. `v2.program`'s run (`v2.workflow.runtime_run`) consumes the derived activation (`v2.compiler.activation_dependency`): it evaluates a site's selector, has the interpretation choose, and evaluates only the chosen alternative, with eval's own admission and result checks. `v2.program` builds its tree with no resolve phase, so it cannot consume value-use relations (`v2.compiler.value_use_dependency`); their consumer is the body-evaluator check below, on resolved programs. `program_run_dependencies` is one fixture row, so deleting it proves little on its own: the consumer the section 2.4 rule matters to is the body evaluator (`v2.compiler.eval`), whose `Bind` traversal orders effects today, and M2 is not met until the derived relation is what that evaluator's ordering is checked against. Execution follows the adopted consumer-relative sequence (M2a: derive -> switch `v2.program` -> delete `program_run_dependencies`; M2b: one next consumer); `module_resolution_plan`'s future work is adapting its already-derived import authority to judgment dependencies and replacing its batch realization, not deleting a hand-authored population. Moving ordering authority is itself the transition D16 gates: the body evaluator's order does not switch to the derived relation before D16's checker lands, even with one seat, because a one-seat reorder can already change effect behavior."),
    p(text: "**Qualification.** Derived relations equal the hand rows on every existing specimen before the rows are deleted; a missing-edge mutation makes admission refuse instead of reading as independence; an undeclared effect refuses concurrent realization at the realization layer, not in the engine; a selected arm's read set differs by branch and yields different evaluation identities."),
    p(text: "**Completeness before independence.** A consumer reads the whole derivation, including its typed not-derived dispositions (`v2.compiler.value_use_dependency` `ValueUseDependencies`, and the activation derivation's equivalent). A non-empty not-derived population over what it schedules refuses; only a complete admitted population supports an independence conclusion, and an absent edge is never read as one. An untaken alternative contributes no runtime read, refusal or effect, while every alternative is still judged at compile time. A `Node` endpoint is not the runtime demand key: a demand is a judgment over structure and context (section 2.2), so two instances of one template under different contexts never merge."),
    p(text: "**Specimens (operator, 2026-10-18).** Each is qualified against the derived relation M2's consumers read, not a lens-only assertion, and no effect specimen may pass because of the interpreter's left-to-right `Bind` traversal, which is a realization order (D1), not a source of dependency. Ordered cases run on an arrangement that would expose a missing edge, because passing under a serial evaluator is weak evidence. The engine-level readiness cases (A1 -> B beside an independent A2; distinct fresh-effect productions) stay with M3a. The effect specimens cannot qualify until D16's checker lands under its TRIGGER."),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "**Independent bindings** (`v2.std.datetime` `leap_second_next_day`): no derived relation between any two of its five bound values; reordering the declarations preserves the relation up to renamed identities."),
      li(text: "**Independent reads under an ordered choice** (`gunbc.auth.login_transaction_store` `login_transaction_decode_body`): no relation among the six field reads; with two malformed fields the reported error is unchanged. The nested `Match` orders which result wins, not when anything runs."),
      li(text: "**A real value use:** the consumer waits on the binding's value with no cycle through the enclosing `Bind`; a missing-edge mutation refuses admission."),
      li(text: "**An untaken branch** holding an effect or a refusal stays inactive and contributes no effect, refusal or read."),
      li(text: "**A guarded read after a write** (`gunbc.deploy_transition` `set_transition_inhibition`; likewise `gunbc.host.host_effect_realize` `srv3_chown_then_read_back`): the selected observation is activated by the write's outcome through branch selection, a derived control dependency."),
      li(text: "**Write, then live read:** the read waits for the write's declared completion through the continuation it consumes, wherever it sits in the source."),
      li(text: "**A live read and a write holding one exclusive predecessor:** refused as a conflict; source order does not choose a direction."),
      li(text: "**Old contents read beside a replacement:** admitted only under a snapshot contract that retains the old bytes and accounts for the live publication."),
      li(text: "**Several readers, then a mutation:** the readers may overlap inside one shared phase; the mutation waits for that phase to close."),
      li(text: "**Two writes claimed independent:** they overlap only where disjoint access is established; unresolved aliasing between computed paths refuses rather than reading as independence."),
      li(text: "**A conditional effect:** only the selected alternative consumes the access, and the continuation and failure outcome are checked on every active path."),
      li(text: "**A timed computation** (`v2.compiler.source_root_eval` `native_demand_step`): the start observation precedes the work's activation and the end observation follows its declared completion, so the timed operation controls activation; ordering the two clock reads alone brackets nothing."),
      li(text: "**A long-lived or uncertain external effect:** access stays accounted for after a spawn returns or a call times out, and a requested effect stays demanded when its ordinary result is unused."),
      li(text: "**Pointwise work spelled as a fold** (`v2.std.dependency` `dependency_view_relations`, `v2.lens.parallelism` `parallelism_fact_unresolved`): once spelled pointwise, members carry no relation between them; a non-associative fold keeps its carried state. This specimen waits on `gunbc.plans.for_while_loop_sugar`'s carrier-free encoding, unbuilt today: `v2.compiler.fold_lowering` still lowers `map`, `filter`, `any` and `all` to a carrier recurrence. The carrier encoding `fold_lowering` builds is representation only: no interpreter executes a `Loop` (`v2.extdeps.runtimes.v2_evaluator` `v2_eval_step_loop` and `v2.program` `program_step_loop` reject every one), the rust target has no `Loop` spelling, and production `fold` runs as `std.algebra` `fold_list`, emitted as a serial loop threading the accumulator, while `map` runs as `list_map` over `fold_list_right`, emitted as recursion. Under M2V's routing (operator, 2026-10-22), `map` is an ordinary resolved call instantiated as a domain demand template over the resolved tree, never routed to a `Loop` encoding, and its recursion route through `list_map` over `fold_list_right` is removed with the M2V cut for the admitted population. The domain-only `Loop` execution half in `gunbc.plans.for_while_loop_sugar` serves surface `for`, not `map`. `filter`, `any` and `all` return different things, so each needs its declared composition first: `filter` retains members in input order, and `any` and `all` wait on D17."),
      li(text: "**Tree traversal** (`v2.std.node` `fold_node` over `NodeFold`): `fold_node` introduces no accumulator-derived sibling dependency between recursive child computations. For an algebra whose complete dependency, activation and effect contract admits independent child evaluation (the completeness rule above), those children carry no sibling prerequisite; the argument list alone does not establish it, since `init` and `step` are arbitrary callbacks. Each child result feeds its own combine, and any actual combine recurrence remains unless M7 licenses regrouping. No all-child prerequisite is added to the semantic graph; a realization may still delay ready work for admitted capacity or grouping, which is not a semantic prerequisite. `NodeFoldDependent` relations are derived from the particular algebra's context reads. The cut joins M6: before a traversal is rewritten, its consumer is named as retained and migrated or as replaced by a judgment cut, and no interim parallel traversal is built for a root M6 deletes."),
      li(text: "**Inference's own sibling dependencies** (`v2.std.node` `fold_node_dependent` as `v2.compiler.infer` uses it): `Bind` value before body and `Match` scrutinee before arms are stated through the algebra's `first` schedule, not inherited from authored child order. Today `infer_gather_schedules_first` states only loop domains and non-callback arguments, so the rest rests on position."),
    ]),
    p(text: "**Where execution changes.** M2 derives and admits relations; it does not by itself change body evaluation order or concurrency (inference's own child schedule already reads its stated `first` relations, and the native fold already reads prerequisites from the engine at its coarser grain, `v2.compiler.source_root_eval` `native_demand_execute`). Ordering authority moves to the engine in two scoped cuts, both one-seat: at M2V for resolved body evaluation of the admitted population (deleting that path's evaluator order and `runtime_run`'s private activation driver), and at M3a for the native-fold driver (deleting its scheduling loop). Materialization and grouping choices become measurement-derived at M4, concurrent execution is M5(a), materialization and recomputation grain is M6, and remaining sequential combines regroup only under law evidence at M7. A slice that lands a derivation or a representation names the cutover that consumes it, and is not reported as that cutover. Since 2026-10-22 a derivation lands only together with that cutover (M2V, no sidecar derivations)."),
    p(text: "**Exit.** No production dependency relation restates derivable program flow."),
    h3(text: "M2V — One vertical semantic path (operator, 2026-10-22)"),
    p(text: "**Why this milestone exists.** The first M2 slices derived correct facts BESIDE the compiler instead of becoming what it reads. `v2.compiler.value_use_dependency` (A1) has claims and a declared future consumer. `v2.compiler.activation_dependency` (A2) is read by one special path, `v2.workflow.runtime_run` (A4). Resolve, infer, eval, emission and the native demand plan each keep their own traversal, and their own order. A3 is the model to follow: one role authority, `v2.std.node`'s slot tables, that every stage reads. Dependencies need that same shape: one derived plan that every stage on a path consumes, with no stage keeping a second order."),
    p(text: "**Program rule: no sidecar derivations.** From here on a derivation lands in the change that cuts a production consumer over to it and deletes the order or representation it displaces. A claim, a lens or an isolated witness is not that consumer. A1-A4 (#14418, #14635, #14636, #14679, #14689) are the front half of M2V, not M2 met."),
    p(text: "**Makes true.** One ordinary resolved function body runs from one derived plan through resolve, infer, interpreted eval and native emitted execution, and no stage on that path treats traversal order or child order as meaning. Concretely:"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "**One binding rule.** A use depends on whatever supplies its binder in its judgment context: a `let` value; a call's actual argument for a formal parameter; the selected component of the scrutinee for a Match payload binder; the domain element for a loop member; the initial value or the previous member's result for a carrier. Resolve already says WHICH binder a use names. The call, activation or iteration instance says WHAT supplies it. A1 is the `let` instance of this rule and generalizes into it rather than gaining sibling modules (DESIGN section 6b, root generalization)."),
      li(text: "**Judgment endpoints.** Relations connect judgments over a node AND its context (section 2.2), keyed by `std.judgment_contract` `DemandIdentity` through `JudgmentContract`, never by raw `Node` equality. Two loop members, or two calls of one function, are different judgments. The Node-instantiated rows of A1 and A2 are lifted to these identities and keep their occurrence and role. No second identity type is minted."),
      li(text: "**One total plan.** Prerequisites (`v2.std.dependency` `DependencyRelation`), activations (`ActivationDemand`), member templates, binding sources and typed not-derived rows travel together, so a consumer cannot read part of the plan and treat an absent edge as independence. The shared carrier lives in `v2.std` because the stages depend on it, but its first production declaration lands in the same cut as its first migrated consumer, and each later extension lands with the consumer that needs it. There is no standalone build-the-plan-first milestone (the no-sidecar rule above)."),
      li(text: "**One carrier, established in order.** The plan is one progressively established authority, not a snapshot taken before resolve. Facts knowable before resolution enter first: structural roles, activation templates and declaration-bound elaboration. Resolve consumes the facts that apply to it and adds what it owns: which binder each use names, and the binding-source obligations that follow. Infer, eval and emission consume the plan as resolve leaves it. A stage may contribute facts whose authority is its own, but it never keeps a second private order beside the plan, and there is no separate pre-resolve plan and post-resolve plan."),
      li(text: "**One engine.** `v2.std.demand_engine` gains activation (a selector's value admits exactly one static alternative) and template instantiation (a domain member instantiates its declared demands and relations). Runtime instantiates subjects and never invents a relation kind. `v2.workflow.runtime_run`'s private Branch/Match driving becomes a receipt adapter over the engine."),
      li(text: "**Elaboration before derivation, in a stated chronology.** (1) Before resolution, a `for` site is known by its grammar and body-producer identity, not by a declaration, and enters as a structural template. (2) Resolve answers each ordinary call head with its exact declaration identity. (3) Call-based elaborations are instantiated from those identities: `map` as a domain template with no carrier; `fold`, `fold_list` and `fold_list_right` as a domain template with an explicit carrier and direction; `filter` as its declared composition. (4) The route is chosen: the canonical demand template is instantiated DIRECTLY over the resolved tree, and the tree is NOT rewritten into a Loop image, so no generated binders need resolving a second time and there is no pre-resolution spelling route beside a post-resolution declaration route. `v2.compiler.fold_lowering`'s spelling-keyed recognition of fold call heads, which runs before resolution, is the displaced authority for the admitted population and is deleted with the cut. (5) Elaboration is sealed before dependency derivation and inference consume it. A false carrier never enters the derivation, because pointwise work never passes through fold semantics. `any` and `all` are NOT in M2V's initial population. They stay on the recurrence until the subcut that elaborates them, and that subcut carries D17's ruling and its own migration evidence."),
      li(text: "**Typed phase standings.** Each contributed population carries a standing that distinguishes not applicable, not derived, producing stage not yet run, producing stage refused, and sealed. The carrier advances through structural population sealed, then resolution contributions sealed, then inference-facing plan sealed, then execution-facing plan sealed. Each consumer requires the standing it needs, so an absent relation in an unsealed population can never read as independence. Once a population is sealed, no later stage may append to it."),
      li(text: "**Stages read the plan; realization may be coarse.** A stage-wide fold or a serial loop may realize the plan only as an admitted fused realization: it carries a checkable record of which judgments it discharges, and preserves every relation, activation, effect and refusal. Fine authority does not require per-node dispatch (section 2.2). Static stages still judge every alternative: resolve and infer visit untaken arms, and only eval activates exactly one."),
    ]),
    p(text: "**Population and effects (operator, 2026-10-22).** The first population is effect-free bodies, admitted by one rule that reuses existing authorities. The body's complete direct and transitive closure comes from D13's derived dependency demand (`v2.compiler.dependency_demand`: its call facts and fixed point, with Undecided absorbing), so no second call walk is built. Each operation reached is joined to its `v2.compiler.effect_demand` `PrimitiveExecutionSemantics`. `Pure` is admitted. `ReadsBoundInput` is admitted when its input binding is complete, since it needs no host boundary. `ObservesHost`, `ActuatesHost` and `ExecutionSemanticsUnknown` refuse. An opaque declaration is judged by its explicit contract. An unresolved callee or an incomplete closure refuses with a typed disposition. A D13 resource set is evidence for D16's access model, not effect reach: `RequiresNone` declares no resource requirement and proves nothing about effects. A refused body is refused, typed and located, and never inherits written order. **Modeling obligation found by the B0 census design (2026-10-22).** `effect_demand`'s execution-semantics roster classifies only a handful of operations, keyed by a primitive slug, while D13 keys every operation by its declaration path, and no declared join connects the two keys: one concept, operation identity, has two keyings (DESIGN section 3). Until each operation declares its execution semantics where it declares its resource requirement, keyed by that same declaration path, an operation with no semantics refuses as `ExecutionSemanticsUnknown`. The effect-free population is therefore small at first, and the census measures it. The remedy is that declaration, not a wider default. This keeps D16's gate: no effect is reordered before D16's checker lands. The same structural rule is the admission source for `map`'s first executing cut (`gunbc.plans.for_while_loop_sugar` section 2.6). It is a temporary, conservative population gate. Effectful bodies join M2V's population through D16's sequence: census, rulings on parts (1)-(5), the access model, the checker landing together with the corpus migration, then the switch. **D16 enriches this same plan.** Its derived access subjects, its conflict and order relations, its continuation and completion obligations, and its unresolved and not-derived dispositions enter the one total plan. There is no parallel effect-order graph and no second scheduler input. When those facts exist, they replace the structural gate as the admission and order authority: they admit an effectful member once its access relations are known, which is not a test of effect-freedom."),
    p(text: "**Census before cutting.** A `gunbc test` instrument classifies every traversal on the path -- each `fold`, `fold_list`, `fold_node` family use, direct `children` walk and positional read in resolve, infer, eval, target and emission -- as structural enumeration, explicit recurrence, admitted fused realization of the plan, or unresolved. Unresolved blocks the cut. A traversal is an admitted fused realization only when an actual plan-discharge record exists for it. A legacy fold intended to become one is unresolved until then, so the census never pre-approves the structure it is meant to challenge. The census names what each cut deletes; it is re-derived, never transcribed."),
    p(text: "**Deletes in the same change.** For the admitted population: eval's own child-order traversal, kept neither as a fallback nor as an oracle on the production path; `runtime_run`'s private activation driving; `map`'s route through `list_map` over `fold_list_right`; and the Node-only A1 and A2 entry points the plan supersedes."),
    p(text: "**Production consumer.** Resolved body evaluation in `v2.compiler.eval`, and native emitted execution of the same body. This is the consumer A1 named. M2's earlier wording, the evaluator's order merely CHECKED against the derived relation, is superseded for the admitted population: the plan GOVERNS that order."),
    p(text: "**Qualification.** One source-to-native specimen, `fold(xs, 0, step)` where `step(acc, x)` binds `y = expensive_pure(x)` and answers `acc + y`, establishes from the one plan that: each `expensive_pure(x_i)` has no relation to another; each addition depends on its `y_i` and on the carrier; carrier `i` comes from result `i-1`; inference sees the same formal and member bindings; the interpreter's one-seat realization and native emission both respect the plan; and a shuffled completion of the independent demands leaves the whole result contract unchanged (values, refusal selection, diagnostics). Mutations, each required RED: removing the carrier edge breaks the recurrence claim; adding a predecessor edge between the pure calls breaks the independence claim; inserting an unresolved effect makes admission refuse rather than serialize by index; reading a binder's producer by position breaks a shared-node control. **Per-stage consumer pairing.** For every stage the cut claims as migrated, an executing control reaches that stage through the shared plan, and a mutation that disconnects that stage's read of the plan, or restores its displaced private order, turns that control red. One end-to-end specimen may qualify several stages if its mutations discriminate each stage's integration independently. A fused stage also proves its discharge record against the plan population it claims to realize. The traversal census is needed for deletion completeness, but it is not consumption evidence and does not replace this pairing. **Population differential.** The fold specimen is the mutation oracle, but it does not qualify the replacement population. Before any old route is deleted, the admitted body population is enumerated at identity grain, and branch and main run over that same population. The complete `JudgmentContract` outcome is compared: semantic value, refusal selection, diagnostics in canonical order, activation population, effects and receipts where present, and provenance obligations. Every difference is classified as unchanged, an explicitly ruled semantic migration (for example `map`'s callback and refusal order), a new effect-gate refusal, a pre-existing refusal, or unattributed or unexecuted. The unattributed and unexecuted populations must be empty. **Refusal contract in the effect-free population.** Whether a member after a refusing member is computed is unobservable except as cost. The observable contract is fixed: the lowest-index refusal is the one selected, and later members' advisory diagnostics are not reported. D17 governs as soon as that activation becomes observable."),
    p(text: "**Exit.** On the admitted population, no compiler stage holds a dependency or an order that the plan does not state."),
    h3(text: "M3a — Native-fold authority cut (one-seat in-process)"),
    p(text: "**Makes true.** Section 2.5 with Share and explicit Recompute on the native fold: demand closure, suspension, availability states, one ready queue, one dispatch policy, typed blocked states, in-flight attachment, one-seat in-process realization. The native fold's driver becomes a `.dag` demand over its universe; the emitted main reduces to the host-effect boundary. M3a deletes the native fold's SCHEDULING loop only. The current interpreter remains the production semantic handler until emitted Rust implements the complete judgment and refusal contract."),
    p(text: "**Deletes in the same change.** The Rust module loop in `v1.compiler.emit_rust` `emit_source_root_eval_driver_main_rs`. The interpreter is not deleted and is not demoted to an oracle at this cut."),
    p(text: "**Production consumer.** The v2 native fold (landing receipts) -- the first production root that switches to the engine and loses its manual scheduling loop."),
    p(text: "**Qualification.** Byte-identical driver rows against the pre-cutover fold on the bounded concurrent A/B harness and one capped exact-head full fold; undemanded judgments never run; B becomes READY immediately after A1 becomes available, without waiting for A2 (mutation delaying A1's availability keeps B waiting); equal share-admitted pure demands yield one production and multiple attachments (mutation produces twice); fresh-effect invocations remain distinct productions; world reads share only under an admitted observation/staleness identity; idempotent effects share or retry only under their declared idempotency contract; a refused prerequisite never unblocks; each typed blocked state reachable. `v2.std.demand_engine` `completion_order` records the settlements it was handed, in the order they happened, and `v2.test.claim.demand_engine.demand_engine` `completion_order_records_what_settled_holds` checks that recording property. The receipt keeps recording what actually happened -- every production and settlement event, with its multiplicity, since a fresh effect may be produced more than once on one demand entry -- and is never rebuilt from the derived relation, which says what was REQUIRED and so would read the same for a correct run and a violating one. Once more than one demand runs at once, qualification checks the recorded evidence against the relation: each required predecessor reaches its declared completion boundary before its dependent activates. Two runs are compared up to that partial order, so incomparable demands may settle in different orders between runs, and no check pins one total order across runs."),
    p(text: "**Context-assembly cut, first named instance: the bare-reference edge index of an entry-subject run.** Section 1 defers amending this milestone until the context producer, child judgment grain, displaced loop and bypass discriminator are named; for this one row of `context` they are. PRODUCER: `v1_compiler.cli_run` `build_both_closure_edge_index`, one row per demanded source, reached through the loader fixpoint of `load_sources_for_entry_with_pool`; the run-level preparation is `warm_bare_reference_edge_index_for_entries`. CHILD JUDGMENT GRAIN: one source file -- its bare-reference admission (`admit_bare_references_of_file`) and its reference and bare edge row. DISPLACED LOOP: the fold over every pool file in `whole_pool_closure_edge_index`, as `claim_batch` demanded it before any entry regardless of subject. It was authored duplication of nothing rather than recurrence -- rows produced for no consumer -- so it is deleted for that subject, not materialized. BYPASS DISCRIMINATOR: the run's subject. Explicitly named entries demand the union of their closures; a subject that is the tree (the required floor, compile-clean, a discovery roster) keeps the whole-pool demand. It is observable on the `[floor-phase] phase=bare-reference-edge-index-warm` line as `scope=` with `source_files` against `pool_files`, and an undemanded file holds no row (`closure_edge_demand_tests` `entry_preparation_covers_the_closure_union_and_leaves_refusals_to_their_entry`). PRE-REGISTERED AND PARTLY FALSIFIED: the row count fell to the closure union and every verdict was unchanged, but wall fell by less than the row ratio and resident memory barely moved. The reading that the rows were the cost was therefore wrong in part: each per-file judgment reads pool-wide name information (`pool_parse`, `closure_name_census`, `tree_bare_census_for_root`), which is tree-scale whatever the subject. That residue, and the other tree-scale work outside every resolve span, is now attributed rather than inferred: `claim_batch` prints `[process-partition]` (contiguous spans of the process wall), `[process-partition-exit]`, and the `[pre-entry]` rows of `v1_compiler.cli_run.pre_entry_phase` with their declared scale. Re-derive every figure from those lines on a `claim_batch --claim-run` over named entries; none is restated here. NEXT DERIVATION, not ruled: the rows that partition names as tree-scale for a closure-scale subject -- the module-graph import-edge facts built with the index, the pool name census, the output-policy read, the unimported-bare-provider check -- are each the same question as this one (does an entry-subject run have a consumer for the pool-wide form), and each needs its own producer, grain, loop and discriminator named before it is cut. A per-name census authority already exists (`pool_census_for_name`), so the name-census row is the first candidate."),
    p(text: "**Exit.** No native-fold production program run outside the engine; the interpreter still implements the live judgment until the emitted-Rust completeness trigger."),
    h3(text: "M3b — Remaining scheduler migration and deletion"),
    p(text: "**Makes true.** `v2.program` and `module_resolution_plan` run on the engine. Named graph-artifact authorities relocate: `NodeKeyedGraphArtifact`, `NodeKeyedGraphRow`, `NodeKeyedGraphArtifactEntry`, intern/lookup rows, `node_keyed_graph_transitive_bytes` and the missing-row refusal arms (`NodeKeyedGraphRowMissing`, `NodeKeyedGraphTransitiveBytesMissingRow`)."),
    p(text: "**Deletes in the same change.** `v2.std.dependency` `topological_layers` (a depth-first serialization, not a readiness schedule) and its scheduling consumers, while structural readers of `Contains` stay; unconsumed `v2.workflow.scheduler` layer planning, `v2.workflow.executor` batches, `v2.workflow.realization_runner` scheduling surfaces (`runner_dispatch_job`, `runner_dispatch_jobs`, `runner_dispatch_batch`, `runner_walk_batches`, `RunnerSchedulePlan`, `runner_schedule_plan_from_dependencies`, `runner_schedule_store_covers_nodes`, `runner_schedule_batch_count`, `runner_schedule_contains_batch`, `runner_execution_dependencies` as a scheduler wrap), and `v2.workflow.runtime_run`'s sequential fold -- after an exact producer/consumer/disposition census reaches zero old scheduling consumers."),
    p(text: "**Production consumer.** `v2.program` and `module_resolution_plan` (and their mirrored host realization), once M3a holds the native fold."),
    p(text: "**Qualification.** Census of old scheduling consumers is empty; relocated graph-artifact authorities still refuse missing refs and still derive transitive bytes; no remaining production walk of antichain batches."),
    p(text: "**Exit.** No layer or batch executor remains as a production consumer."),
    h3(text: "M4 — Measured demand economics"),
    p(text: "**Makes true.** Receipts join to demand and realization identities: semantic work, realization overhead, encoding and lookup, expected reuse, retention and resource footprint, as `std.realization_schedule` `CostAccount` axes per demand class; the ladder's exempt roster is replaced by a measured serve-versus-recompute decision."),
    p(text: "**Deletes in the same change.** `cost_floor_exempt` roster entries replaced by measured decisions."),
    p(text: "**Production consumer.** The M3a engine's materialization choice for the native fold."),
    p(text: "**Qualification.** A planted demand whose dispatch overhead exceeds its work is not split. Economics are conditional on admitted handlers: if a qualifying memoize/store provider exists, economics may select memoize/store; if memoization is economically required but no admitted provider exists, the choice is typed `MemoizeRequiredButNoAdmittedProvider`; if only recompute and share handlers exist, choose among those. A cost receipt missing its realization identity refuses admission to the decision. Economic preference is not realization capability."),
    p(text: "**Exit.** Materialization and grouping choices on the native fold are measurement-derived."),
    h3(text: "M5 — Realization and materialization selection; widths deleted (consumer-relative cuts)"),
    p(text: "**Makes true.** The remaining local-process supply, selection, occupancy and nested-scheduler portions of section 2.7, plus section 2.8, as five consumer-relative replacement migrations -- D9's emitted-crate partition is a prior standalone realization cut and is not owned by M5 -- each its own root deletion with supply AND occupancy (a `SupplierOffer` with no committed lease path is half-wired): (a) native local-child-process realization -- offer + committed lease + handler; deletes that route's old concurrency control; (b) persistent materialization for one native family -- canonical encoding, section 2.8 manifest-validation protocol, content verification; deletes that family's old store path; (c) required-floor discovery/parse sweep as engine demands; deletes `CONTROLLED_WIDTH`, `DiscoveryWidthPolicy`, and the unbounded thread scope for that consumer; (d) cargo/make/jobserver tokens from the committed claim; deletes independent `-j` and serial fallback; (e) residual width census; deletes the last fallback/minimum-one helpers when unconsumed. Also the realization-measurement-loop gate (uncached non-redundant work is a typed error)."),
    p(text: "**Deletes in the same change.** Per cut above: the named old concurrency, store, width-policy, independent jobserver, and residual helper roots -- never a single simultaneous dump of every width integer before its consumer has a lease path."),
    p(text: "**Production consumer.** (a)-(b) the native fold; (c) the required floor; (d) cargo/make under that fold and floor; (e) the residual census of retired width helpers."),
    p(text: "**Qualification.** Observation refusal (missing CPU, memory or PID evidence yields no offer); subunit refusal; conservation (running claims never exceed the grant); two-run conservation on one host; release on spawn failure, refusal, timeout and signal; canonical output under shuffled completion; a verified hit takes no lease; store unavailable refuses; same EvaluationIdentity and same ResultContentIdentity concurrent commit converges on the prior commit after readback verification; same EvaluationIdentity and different ResultContentIdentity is typed impurity/conflict and neither result silently wins; an M-versus-T receipt recomputes only changed evaluation identities (counted); a repository census finds no production consumer of a retired width mechanism; byte-identical rows at every cardinality the offer yields. Dependency conformance does not prove concurrency, because a serial run satisfies every dependency: (a) qualifies only when independent slow demands are observed to overlap under granted capacity, a dependent demand starts only after its prerequisite is available, and no layer barrier appears."),
    p(text: "**Exit.** No feature-authored concurrency width, environment knob, shard count, minimum-one fallback, or refusal-to-serial path remains; concurrent cardinality derives only from admitted resource claims and active leases; every concurrent realization holds a committed lease; cross-run reuse on the landing-receipt path."),
    h3(text: "M6 — Compiler stages as vertical judgment cuts"),
    p(text: "**Makes true.** Semantic verticality -- every stage deriving its dependencies, with traversal order meaning nothing -- is M2V's and is widened there (operator, 2026-10-22). M6 is materialization grain: one complete path at a time -- source demand, parse result, resolution result, inference and eval result, native verdict -- becomes separately keyed, materialized and recomputed judgment demands, its stage-wide fold root deleted, then widened to the next path."),
    p(text: "**Deletes in the same change.** Each stage-wide fold as its judgment form lands."),
    p(text: "**Production consumer.** The native fold, path by path."),
    p(text: "**Qualification.** Byte-identical results per cut; a one-file change recomputes only its dependents (counted); each cut's deleted root has no remaining production consumer."),
    p(text: "**Exit.** On an admitted warm-materialization path, recomputation is limited to changed evaluation identities and their declared transitive dependents, plus explicitly classified fresh/global obligations."),
    h3(text: "M7 — Law-bearing algebraic decomposition"),
    p(text: "**Makes true.** Law evidence carriers under observation contracts, their establishment by inhabitance and witnesses, the canonical append-stable decomposition, and realization applying decomposition only where laws are established and economics favor it. Lands after M6 so it does not optimize stage-wide folds that M6 deletes."),
    p(text: "**Deletes in the same change.** Any convention-level parallelism assumption about remaining sequential combines (none may be introduced before this milestone)."),
    p(text: "**Production consumer.** Remaining sequential combines on the M6 cut paths whose combine carries established evidence -- not the stage-wide folds M6 deletes."),
    p(text: "**Qualification.** Decomposed and sequential results identical over the full observation contract, including diagnostic order; an unestablished-law mutation stays sequential; an observation-changing combine (diagnostic order) is refused decomposition; appending one element recomputes a bounded path (counted)."),
    p(text: "**Exit.** Every production decomposition cites its law evidence."),
    h2(text: "6. Program-wide qualification"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "**Identity of results.** Every engine milestone is byte-identical in driver rows against its predecessor on the bounded concurrent A/B harness, then on one capped exact-head full fold; a difference stops the line."),
      li(text: "**Production-route pairing.** Every hand-specimen or supplied-boundary witness is paired with at least one production-route inhabitance witness. The real producer's value must reach the real consumer through the authority under test, and deleting or bypassing that integration must make the paired control red. Shape agreement alone does not discharge this obligation."),
      li(text: "**Full JudgmentContract equality.** Semantic-equivalence receipts compare the complete `JudgmentContract` result: value, ordered diagnostics, refusal arm and cause, provenance/occurrence contract, effect requests and receipts, and required artifact outputs. Native driver-row byte identity is an additional projection-specific requirement, never the complete generic equality criterion."),
      li(text: "**Discriminating controls.** Every new witness names its mutation and why it reds, and fits the floor's per-identity step budget (hand specimens, not the corpus)."),
      li(text: "**Deletion is the census.** Each milestone runs a delete-first branch through the fail-closed floor before its detailed plan, and lands with a census showing no production consumer of what it retired."),
      li(text: "**Conformance.** Every change is read by `conformance-identity`, `conformance-realization`, `conformance-compute` and `conformance-decision`; departures state reasons. `conformance-identity` owns the key relation and input completeness; `conformance-realization` owns faithful realization of an admitted identity and result contract. Cache purity is one discriminator across that boundary."),
      li(text: "**Slice completion.** A replacement slice is complete only when the real producer reaches its named production consumer through the shared authority, the displaced route is removed for the slice's declared population, the full result and refusal contract is exercised at the reviewed head, and an integration-breaking mutation is detected by the consumer's control rather than only a helper's. Representation-only and derivation-only slices may land as such and name the cutover that consumes them. The required job evaluates no witness, so each slice records the head, the route that ran its claims, claim identities, executed outcomes, pre-existing reds and mutation outcomes; a compile-only run, zero selected claims or a skipped execution discharges nothing. The slice names the executing qualification gate and enrolls its consumer-pairing and regression controls there; where the native gate cannot yet execute that population, the slice names the limitation and its cutover and is not reported as fully qualified. Landing receipts remain evidence, but a plan retires only when its retained obligations pass on the integrated retirement head."),
      li(text: "**Cost is reported, not asserted.** Receipts name the instrument that re-derives them, never transcribed numbers."),
    ]),
    h2(text: "7. Operator decisions required before M1"),
    p(text: "Every decision row is Ruled or Deferred with a trigger. Do not transcribe a count beside the rows; the grep receipt lives in the PR body."),
    ol(items: [
      li(text: "**D1 — What does eval name?** Option A: eval is the semantic judgment and the run engine composes demand, minimization, materialization and realization (DESIGN-consistent as written; the side chat's recommendation). Option B: eval names the whole run (operator's 2026-09-15 framing), which requires restating DESIGN section 3's three-facts wording so semantics remains a separate authority. Recommendation: A, keeping the operator's realization/materialization split verbatim as the two outer layers. **Ruled 2026-09-15: A.** Eval is not pluggable. Demand derivation is a deterministic authority; demand minimization is a deterministic authority; alternative implementations of either may be extensionally equivalent handlers of that one authority, but policy cannot choose a different semantic demand population. Materialization is an interface with admissible handlers; realization is an interface with admissible handlers; policy selects ONLY among those admissible materialization and realization handlers. A strategy that shapes work from graph facts (cost, law evidence, size) is a realization or materialization handler that may regroup, order, place and retain but never changes which judgments are demanded. The production realization this program selects is emitted Rust; the current interpreter remains the production semantic handler until that route implements the complete judgment and refusal contract, after which the interpreter is the offline differential oracle and not a fallback. THE REALIZATION RULE (operator, 2026-10-18): a realization preserves every fact the language guarantees and promises nothing else. The guaranteed facts are values, derived dependency order, branch activation (an untaken alternative activates no semantic demand and contributes no effect, refusal or read-set member; extra internal work is admissible only where it preserves those facts and the judgment's identity and result contract), declared choice priority, result and diagnostic order where the language fixes it (for example by index, `gunbc.plans.for_while_loop_sugar` section 7), and refusals. Work the program leaves unordered carries no order guarantee and may run in a different order on every execution. So a `Branch` may be realized as a jump, a select, predication or an arithmetic encoding only where those facts survive. Eager extra computation (a select that computes both operands) needs proof that computing the inactive operand can neither perform an effect nor refuse; guarded execution (predication that suppresses the inactive operations) needs proof that the target's instruction semantics really suppress them. An integer encoding such as `b + c*(a - b)` needs bounds covering every intermediate operation, the subtraction as well as the product, since either can overflow where the source could not. Floating-point arithmetic selection such as `c*a + (1-c)*b` is not admitted as a general rewrite, because it changes values (0 times infinity is NaN, and rounding moves); a specialized encoding over a restricted domain needs proof under the complete value and observation contract. The source never changes, so an internal 0/1 encoding is not an implicit `Bool`-to-`Int` coercion (DESIGN section 4)."),
      li(text: "**D2 — The universal demand shape.** `JudgmentContract` keyed through existing node structure, or another equivalent that mints no second AST graph. Recommendation: `JudgmentContract` as the authority, with `ArtifactRequest` only its persistence projection. **Ruled 2026-09-15:** semantic demand and `product.fabric.demand` `Demand<P>` stay distinct carriers with no common parent; a semantic demand placed out of process produces Work and a fabric demand through a named realization projection, not inheritance, and the grain is not one-to-one (in-process realization and materialization hits produce none; retries produce a new fabric attempt for one semantic demand). A hand-maintained universal `JudgmentKind` coproduct beside `ArtifactRequest` is refused; a closed coproduct is admissible only as a derived projection of the contract population."),
      li(text: "**D3 — Key vocabulary.** Adopt section 2.3 identities (structural node, demand, read set, evaluation, result content, occurrence scope) plus EvaluationResultBinding, with collision-resistant families for cross-run evaluation identity and declared collision disposition per scope. Recommendation: adopt the split. **Ruled 2026-09-15: adopted**, including the evaluation / result-content split: same evaluation identity with different content identities is impurity, never convergence."),
      li(text: "**D4 — Runtime facts in a planned graph.** Static demand schemas, dynamically admitted instances. The compiler derives every template, alternative and typed membership slot; runtime invents no dependency kind. `Branch`, `Match` and `Loop` are static demands with a runtime selector; a host call is a demand at its call site whose result is a value, opaque inside; members of a collection that arrives from outside instantiate concrete demands through their declared template and are admitted by the one engine. The engine is opaque to effect payloads and consumes only the generic prerequisite relations and share admissions projected from declarations. The only runtime protocol is suspension on an unavailable selector or member. What remains one layer down, at the host-effect boundary in realization: how a demand that performed an effect and then suspends on a member is resumed. Options: resume in place (a continuation in the emitted program), replay under a declared idempotency contract, or refuse the shape with a typed diagnostic. Recommendation: refuse the shape until resume-in-place exists in emitted Rust; replay only under a declared idempotency contract; restart across a performed effect is never admitted. **Ruled 2026-09-15:** the schema/admission protocol above; refuse-until-resume at the host-effect boundary. Admission is a capability join -- the judgment's effect contract with the selected realization's continuation capability -- decided before the shape starts executing; the engine receives only the resulting verdict."),
      li(text: "**D5 — Law authority.** A law is a typed evidence value (`AssociativeUnder<Obs>`, `CommutativeUnder<Obs>`, `IdempotentUnder<Obs>`, `HomomorphismBetween`) that only the decomposition rule consumes; semantics never depends on one, and an absent law leaves the chain sequential -- a speed loss, never a correctness loss. Each is established at an honestly reported rung (DESIGN section 4b): a witness over a discriminating corpus with a planted non-associative red establishes it on the tested inputs (rung 2, the route for user-declared operations); an exhaustive witness over a small finite carrier (rung 3); structural derivation from lawful parts -- product of monoids, pointwise lift, max and min over a total order, set union (rung 3-4, the route for std composites); or citation of the modeled framework for the standard structures (rung 3). A law-evidence value carries its operation, domain, observation contract, evidence rung and applicability bound: a sampled witness authorizes decomposition only within its carried bound (the tested corpus or a range it structurally contains); exhaustive finite evidence requires a total completeness authority and, for associativity, every triple; structural derivation consumes law evidence for every premise behind a construction wall, so the record is not publicly forgeable; a citation establishes the abstract law and needs a separate inhabitance route showing the live operator is that operation under this representation. When evidence is absent or does not cover the exact subject, execution stays sequential. The observation contract -- value only, or value plus diagnostic population and order, effect requests and order, provenance, refusal shape -- is owned by the judgment's result interface, never selected by a consumer: a consumer may demand a distinct value-only projection but cannot narrow the producer's contract by ignoring axes it still returns. Recommendation: land the evidence types and the refuse-without-evidence rule in `std.algebra` at M7, witnesses as the first producers, structural derivation for std composites. **Ruled 2026-09-15:** that authority and landing milestone."),
      li(text: "**D6 — Canonical decomposition.** Content-defined chunks under a Merkle hierarchy, or a persistent sequence with canonical branching. Recommendation: decide in M7 with measurements; the requirement (bounded invalidation on append) is fixed now. **Deferred:** trigger is M7 measurements choosing the tree; the requirement is not reopened."),
      li(text: "**D7 — Host-global grant home** on hosts running several realizing runs: reuse runner-slot allocation or fabric cell reservation, or model a host ledger. Recommendation: reuse, decided by M5's census. **Deferred:** trigger is M5's occupancy census naming the grant home."),
      li(text: "**D8 — First authority cut.** The v2 native fold's driver (M3a). Recommendation: confirm. **Deferred:** trigger is M3a's detailed plan confirming that driver as the first production scheduling cut; the consumer is not changed here."),
      li(text: "**D9 — Crate partition of the emitted closure; generator promotion is a separate fact.** Two facts joined through generation identity, not one decision (side-chat ruling, 2026-09-15). GENERATOR PROMOTION -- seed (v1 emitting v2) versus self-hosted (v2 emitting itself) -- is an admission root cut governed by DESIGN §7 and D1: the seed remains the production generator until the self-hosted route satisfies the complete judgment and refusal contract, then the root cuts over; cost never keeps the seed as a semantic fallback, and it is not a `std.decision` candidate. PARTITION SELECTION is a realization selection over the closure the ACTIVE generator produced: hard constraints first (every module owned exactly once; every source and reference edge represented; complete `CompilationUnit` interface closure; Rust R4 validity; no illegal private access or impl ownership; the stable facade; complete semantic and refusal equivalence), then `std.decision` `select_realization` over a bounded declared candidate field -- one crate (the baseline, which remains a candidate), runtime + remainder, a few derived target-count cuts via `PartitionPolicy`, the policy-derived cut, SCC-per-unit as the diagnostic extreme -- funded on clean emitted-closure build wall, warm incremental wall over a declared edit population (a leaf, a high-fan-in foundation module, a large ordinary compiler module), total CPU, peak memory and process count, link/metadata/process overhead, artifact and binary size, and emission/workspace-construction wall; scoped by closure identity, producer compiler, toolchain, build profile, machine shape, candidate-field identity, evidence snapshot and policy; result `SelectedWithin`, never 'optimal'. At self-host cutover the same authority and decision re-run over the new closure; the prior selection is evidence, not an inherited answer. MEASUREMENT: the reading taken so far is a route wall partition -- seed workspace build, closure emission and one-crate emitted build as three walls read from the native route's build-log timestamps (`build.sh` in the srv1 A/B harness is the producer) -- not a rustc phase partition; `[native-prepare-split]` measures native-driver prepare, not rustc, and is not this instrument. The trigger is therefore the direct candidate A/B: each admitted candidate built from the same emitted closure, toolchain, profile, features and warm shared dependency target, recording per-crate start/end wall, ready/running crate concurrency, total wall and CPU, peak RSS and process count, link wall and artifact size; then the three edit classes with the crate DAG's PREDICTED rebuilt set pre-registered against cargo's observed rebuilt set. Pre-registered structural consequences if a derived cut is right: more than one admitted crate is ready during a nontrivial part of the build; cargo overlaps their compilation; observed rebuilt crates equal the predicted downstream closure after each edit; semantic outputs and refusals identical; the selected candidate wins under the declared field. Line share is not a bound on front-half time and is not a prediction. SEQUENCE: this is a standalone native-route realization cut with a current production consumer (the emitted native closure build) and a current displaced route (the one-crate writer), depending on no engine, fabric supply, cross-run store or width deletion -- it does NOT wait for M5. Its serial position is after M1.a (the JudgmentContract cut) and before M1.b (the keying-relation cut) and M1.c (the cross-run hit cut): it precedes M1.c because the selected `RustCratePartitionIdentity` is part of the compiler artifact's build configuration and therefore of its evaluation identity from the first persistent implementation; if the candidate field selects the one-crate baseline, D9 records that `SelectedWithin` result, performs no physical cut, and the sequence continues; if the runner envelope cannot honestly bound multi-crate cargo concurrency, the authority and A/B still complete and execution defers to M5 -- no `-j` setting belongs here. **Deferred:** trigger = the edge-provenance classification of the emitted reference graph and the candidate A/B above; until both exist this row admits no implementation. **Declared frontier waiting on this row (DESIGN §3c): cross-run materialization of the per-file pool heads reading.** Every entry-subject run re-derives the heads reading of every pool file (`v1_compiler.cli_run.pool_acquire` `heads_reading_for`; the `[pre-entry]` rows `pool_source_tokenize`, `pool_source_newline_index` and `pool_heads_parse`) because the declared-name index it feeds is a tree-subject fact each new process demands for the same tree: recurrence across processes, not authored duplication. Its request is a sibling variant of `std.materialization_provider` `ParseModuleRequest` with the same key fields (`source_digest`, `compiler`) and its own kind and output roster, the persisted value spelling-free with spans rebound on load, stored in the bounded local store under a per-family budget sized so the whole pool fits. It is not built, because its `compiler` input must be the identity of the tokenizer and heads parser alone: the running-binary hash re-keys on every commit, and a lane-local crate digest would be a second crate-identity mechanism beside this row. TRIGGER: D9's crate partition identity exists AND the front-end unit (`v1_compiler_tokenize` and `v1_compiler_parse`, below `v1_infer`) is a partition crate, so that crate's identity is the `compiler` input. CASE ON FILE, a one-off probe of 2026-10-03 and not an instrument (uncommitted test, release build, BuildBuddy runner, the live `dag` plus `src/v2` pool of 7417 files): recomputing every heads reading took 22.5 s (16.2 s under a timer that then covered lexing AND the newline index together, heads parse 6.3 s); reloading them from per-file `serde_json` took 3.3 s (read 0.12 s from a warm page cache, deserialize 3.17 s); 7417 of 7417 reloaded readings compared equal; the JSON was 777 MB for 98 MB of source. WHAT THE PROBE DID NOT TEST: it persisted and reloaded the reading AS IS, with the file spelling still in every span, so its equality count and its reload time are evidence about that form only. The proposed form (spelling-free, spans rebound on load) was not built, not compared and not timed; no pool file had a heads refusal, so a persisted refusal was never exercised. Owed before any build, and unmeasured: an executing comparison, over the live pool, of a fresh reading against the spelling-free persisted value after rebinding, covering the module node, diagnostic and refusal spans, and the file-local intern strings and occurrence-allocator end; the measured cost of that rebinding; the size of the spelling-free form (if it stays near that figure, a compact encoding through `std.materialization_object`'s versioned encoding rather than JSON), one cold-disk read, and the family budget declared from that measurement. The tree name census is a second candidate keyed over the heads digests of a source root's closure; it waits on this one, and `UniverseArtifactRequest` is checked as its family before any new variant."),
      li(text: "**D10 — Native production admission: when the v2-compiled route becomes the production semantic route.** Operator direction (2026-09-15): anchor on the native route now and root-cause disagreements over time; v1 is not the semantic oracle -- the authority is the modeled `JudgmentContract`, and a v1/v2 disagreement is evidence demanding investigation, with neither side winning by age. Two cuts stay separate: native v2 EXECUTION becoming the production semantic route (this row) and the self-hosted GENERATOR replacing the seed (D9 / §7, which still requires the complete generation judgment and refusal contract; a native evaluator may be production while the seed still generates it). Admission is a typed whole-route verdict, `NativeProductionAdmission = NativeProductionAdmitted { contract_identity, required_subject_denominator, result_contract_coverage, refusal_census, divergence_dispositions } | NativeProductionRefused { causes }`, requiring: a named denominator of the judgments and subjects the route promises to serve; total outcomes -- every demanded subject yields an admitted result or an attributed typed refusal; zero unattributed refusals; no contract-violating acceptance; no contract-violating refusal inside the required denominator; every v1/v2 divergence classified against the independent contract or source authority; and no interpreter fallback after the cut. A v2 refusal where v1 accepted is worked after cutover only when the refusal is correct under the contract or the subject lies outside the declared denominator with that boundary explicit -- deferring a false refusal of a valid required subject is not a complete production handler. After cutover, a red parity or admission result latches `NativeProductionRevertRequired` or stops new admissions and awaits an explicit operator disposition; it never silently re-executes the judgment through v1 (the current routing consumer's fall-back-to-interpreted arm is exactly the escape hatch this row deletes). Recommendation: adopt, and run the pre-anchor census of §1 as its first instrument. **Deferred:** trigger is that census reaching zero unattributed refusals with every divergence dispositioned, at which point the admission verdict is computed for the declared denominator; the interpreter-remains-production sentence in M3a is retired by this row's `NativeProductionAdmitted` and by nothing else."),
      li(text: "**D11 — Effects on the native route: the minimal set, and callability. Ruled 2026-09-16.** Scope (operator): Filesystem plus the Gap-B boundary of section 2.9 (argv, stdout/stderr, and the existing elapsed and peak-resident measurement seams) are realized in the emitted closure through bound realizations returning values; Clock, Network, Entropy and AuthContext are not realized by this program -- emitted interfaces refused by `NativeEffectRealizationAdmission`, outside D10's denominator; a scope ruling, not a drop. Callability (side-chat ruling, refined option (a)): resource operations are admissible in ORDINARY functions only through an explicit effect/resource requirement -- an effect envelope or capability parameter on the function -- and the entry realization supplies the matching handler; no ordinary function acquires an ambient global capability merely because a native handler exists. Option (b), entry folds as workflow functions, is refused: it makes 'workflow function' the price of any effect and pulls the workflow realization into every native entry, conflating a semantic effect requirement with one orchestration realization -- against this program's terminal goal of ordinary sequential programs receiving lawful realization for free. E1 falsifies the emitter's implementation of this ruling; it does not reopen it. The fork that option (b) would have deepened -- `uses` read as workflow identity -- is resolved by D12 below: the workflow-function concept is DELETED, not separated. There is no marker and no function-kind authority; executable-entry identity is the artifact-relative `compiler_pipeline_entry` (an executable has exactly one, a library none, and more than one or an unresolved driver refuses), never a property of a function; `uses` keeps its one meaning as an effect requirement; and sync/async derives from effects plus the bound handler, which the emitter was measured to key on `has_services` and `uses`, never on any entry role. **D11 terminal** (a condition of D10 and of this plan's dissolution): ordinary-function effect callability is implemented through explicit contracts; every in-denominator host effect has exactly one admitted native handler; unsupported effects produce typed admission refusals; the direct per-driver implementations those handlers displace are deleted; and the no-interpreter controls execute on the production route. A terminal D10 may not be reached by choosing a denominator that sidesteps this obligation. **Declared extension for native SERVICE entries (gentle-dove-36, 2026-10-04, extending this operator ruling of 2026-09-16):** a `NativeServeDriver` entry (first consumer the approval broker) is a different denominator from the compiler route, and in it Clock and Network are admitted; Entropy and AuthContext stay out, and the compiler route keeps this ruling whole. No transport is added: the broker's clock is the `extdeps.clock` `Clock` service on the existing shell transport and its outgoing HTTP is a `transport rest` service on the existing REST lowering, so each is admitted as the one handler `bind_operation_transport` already binds. The carrier, both denominators and the emission refusal are `v1.compiler.emit` `NativeEffectRealizationAdmission`, computed by `v1.compiler.emit_rust` `native_entry_effects`, and both native mains bind exactly the handlers it admits."),
      li(text: "**D12 -- Executable composition root and legacy workflow deletion. Ruled 2026-09-17 (operator + side-chat A').** Workflow was never a semantic kind: it was an emitter heuristic (`v1.compiler.emit_rust` `is_workflow_item`, true for `uses` present, a service or resource name in the registry, or a registry miss) whose sole product was the clap subcommands `emit_main_rs` rendered. The census that grounds the ruling: every built binary is single-entry through `compiler_pipeline_entry` and pulls no workflow function into its main; a closure with no entry already renders the typed exit-2 `emit_entry_point_absent_main_rs`; `v1.compiler.infer` gates no resource operation on workflow, since admissibility is the `uses` clause; and the `is_async` signature field had no reader. So deletion breaks no production binary. Entry identity is an artifact-relative RELATION carried by `compiler_pipeline_entry`; a multi-command surface, when one is demanded, is explicit dispatch data as `gunbc.cli_dispatch_surface` models it, not a function kind. The cut deletes `is_workflow_item`, `collect_workflow_funcs`, `WorkflowFunc`, `to_workflow_func`, `validate_workflow_param_defaults`, the `emit_main_rs` workflow arm, the imported-data-reference default folding that only that arm consumed, and the dead `is_async` field; it keeps `compiler_pipeline_entry` and the entry-absent refusal, and leaves `uses` as an effect requirement -- the legacy workflow population is not marked. Enrolled discriminators (`test.claim.entry_authority_witness`): adding a `uses` requirement or a service call does not change the entry population, so reintroducing `uses`, a service name or a registry miss as entry identity turns a regression control red; one declared entry is the sole root beside an effect; two declarations and an unmodeled driver each refuse. The witnesses whose subject was folding an imported data reference into a CLI default are deleted with the path, because that folding had no other consumer. This SUPERSEDES the D12-marker / FunctionRole direction and its migration of the legacy workflow population. D13 below refines the remaining `uses`-as-carrier sentence: for transparent bodies the requirement is derived, not authored."),
      li(text: "**D13 -- Derived dependency demand and provider binding. Ruled 2026-09-18 (operator).** This SUPERSEDES the E1b 'add uses to every call chain' migration -- that migration is not run. For a transparent (visible-body) function, dependency demand is DERIVED from resolved dependency and operation references, not authored in the header. Direct demand is produced once by resolution; transitive demand is derived over resolved call relations. The qualified carrier is DependencyDemand (the R1/#11401 derive-once-carry pattern): produced once by resolution, not re-read per backend. Dependency identity includes the logical SUBJECT (`source_tree` is not `output_tree`), not only the interface type. Opaque declarations (no body) carry explicit dependency contracts. Policy (may-not-use-Network, upper bounds) constrains the derived set; it is not an asserted use-list. Composition roots bind demanded subjects to admitted providers. Targets may closure-convert derived demands into explicit params or a typed environment. No source operation reaches an ambient host by name; absent or ambiguous provider is a typed refusal. Existing `uses` rows are classified before migration (USES-0); restating rows are deleted because they are derived, and binder / opaque / policy rows move to their owning carriers. A local `std::fs` impl is admitted as ONE Filesystem provider, not a second source-level operation surface. D13 refines D11's uses-as-carrier: the requirement is DERIVED for transparent bodies, authored only for opaque declarations, distinct subjects, and policy envelopes. USES-0 is the identity-grain join of authored `uses` rows against resolved direct-plus-transitive demand -- not a count equality -- and it decides the split; no `uses` row is added to a call chain and no `uses` row is deleted until that census is dispositioned. The analysis that holds the join is `docs/plans/uses-occurrence-census.md`. E2 shape under this ruling: CLI code references `source_tree.list` / `source_tree.try_read`; resolution binds the `source_tree` SUBJECT; the compiler derives direct and transitive DependencyDemand; the NativeCliDriver root binds LocalFilesystem (impl Filesystem: try_read -> `std::fs::read`, list -> `read_dir`); realization threads it; `std::fs` leaves main; tests bind InMemoryFilesystem. No bare `filesystem_try_read` / `filesystem_list` globals. `emit_file_call` stays renderer machinery reused internally, not the dependency model and not a handler."),
      li(text: join(["**D13 follow-up population.** Cut (c) measured its population, then main added more `uses` rows while it was in flight; those were carried unmeasured until a final-base census over ", demand_restatement_follow_up_commit_range as String, " measured every authored row in the tree and, under the same criterion, deleted each one whose derived demand was nonempty EXCEPT the identities explicitly retained under EditedWitnessHasNoHermeticRoute. None had empty demand, so no function flipped from effectful to pure. What remains is two kinds of row, each named at the row identity USES-0 defined and each carrying the trigger that is true for IT rather than for its module. Rows the criterion cannot type: ordinary fns whose module does not resolve, one refusal probe that never will, and pattern declarations the criterion never ranged over. And rows MEASURED DELETABLE whose deletion is not yet landable: wet witnesses whose edit the floor would plan onto a hermetic route that has no arm for their operation. The retained rows, each with its trigger: ", demand_restatement_retained_row_texts() |> join(separator: "; "), "."], "")),
      li(text: "**D14 -- Environmental closure by ensure. Ruled 2026-10-01 (operator, session wonderful-feynman).** This SUPERSEDES the 'program runtimes as derived prerequisites' D14 draft and the requirement-obligation vocabulary considered in the same session (RequirementObligation, a public RequirementClosure graph beside the demand graph, DependencyDemand read as an obligation, a universal ExecProposition coproduct, an authored OperandSlot); none of those names is minted. AMENDED later the same day (operator ruling: no keyword for what the structure already states): the organizing law is completion, residual and closure -- the compiler completes a partial program from types, sole constructors, realizations, catalogs, root policy and registered providers; what it cannot derive is a typed residual addressed to the role that answers it (call site, root, upstream module, observer); a library may stay open and an executable must close; the one semantic addition is elaboration of omitted operands, D13 generalized to every operand, with no syntax, plus two admission obligations D13 does not carry and this row states so they are not smuggled in as widening -- a derivable-operand rule over COMPLETE applications (an operand whose type has a completion route must be omitted or supplied with a stated divergence), and lifecycle execution provenance, which is established by the library's sealed carriers and the domain mint's admission rather than by demand derivation, at the mechanically-preventable rung the design document states; the `ensure fn` / `ensure` parameter / `ensured` type spellings of the first version are withdrawn as dual representations. Ensure is the library name of one completion mechanism, the modality `gunbc.ensure` already carries: a selected realization consumes only a target-qualified environmental value (`MaterializedProgram`, `PresentPrincipal`, `OwnedDirectory`), never a raw identity; an ensure implementation's typed parameters are its prerequisites, so the compiler resolves them recursively, deduplicates equal targets, derives the DependencyRelations this engine consumes with the operand slot read off the parameter, and seals; a composition root supplies a host blueprint of base observations, provider families with policies, launch environments, principals and the admission envelope, and the compiler closes each executable against it into a derived host closure that is a reviewable projection; a library may stay open, an executable may not, and every host an executable runs on -- the developer's included -- is a placement with a blueprint; only an ensure implementation mints its result, after an effective readback, with its effect footprint exposed and a recovery law admitted before automatic retry; the typed operation contract is the source of ensure demand and the failure model audits it (environmental closure and failure-audit completeness are two standings); ambient state never discharges a target. RE-ROOTED 2026-10-01 (review of #12872): the universal law -- partial programs, maximal lawful completion, typed residuals with addressees, closure at runnable roots, the role of lenses -- is owned by `gunbc.plans.v2_compiler_architecture`, not by this program; this program is a consumer of the completed and closed program and its derived dependency relations, and D14's environmental completion produces qualified operands and relations BEFORE this engine seals. The number D14 is historical; the authority direction is the governance row in `gunbc.plan_governance`. D14 is D13's provider binding applied to environmental state, with the lifecycle (observe, assess, apply, read back, mint) inside one demand node, and it refines D11's process realization: `shell.Exec.RunArgv` takes an `ExecutableCommand` whose runtime is a `MaterializedProgram`, so a bare program spelling cannot reach spawn. The design authority, the git vertical, the census of what it replaces and the landing order are `gunbc.plans.ensure_closure_design`."),
      li(text: "**D15 -- How a native test verdict is produced (native-seven Phase B). Ruled 2026-10-14 (operator).** (a) EVAL: D1 A stands. A test's verdict is the judgment owed for the test node in its context, and running it is realize composed with materialize; the operator's 2026-10-14 wording that eval is realization plus materialization names those outer layers of the run, not a re-ruling to option B. (b) IDENTITY: the semantic evaluation identity (judgment kind, subject node and context, declared inputs, sealed dependency and read set, semantics version) keys the evaluation; the RealizationProductIdentity (handler, generator, target model, toolchain, crate partition, build configuration, packaged declaration set) keys the realized product and is carried on the receipt; result content identity is joined by EvaluationResultBinding, so results from different admitted handlers under one semantic evaluation identity must agree, and different content is impurity (D3). `std.materialization_provider` `NativeModuleVerdictBundleSubject` moves `route_binary_identity` to the realization side and sharpens `eval_interpretation_identity` into the semantic evaluation identity. (c) HANDLERS: the realization handler is a closed coproduct. Emitted Rust is the only admitted production handler; C and Swift inhabit the interface as typed-unavailable arms that refuse, never as selectable candidates that fail later. (d) GRAIN: semantic demand is a judgment over a node plus its context, with one EvaluationResultBinding per test identity; dependencies are edges between judgments, not an identity grain. Module bundles and Cargo crates are aggregation grains for materialization and realization only: `NativeModuleVerdictBundleRequest` is a persisted manifest of per-test bindings plus preparation diagnostics, not the semantic unit, so changing one test leaves every unchanged test's binding addressable. EMISSION GRAIN (ruled 2026-10-14 on the B0 feasibility receipt, which found v2 emission module-grained with 131 of the native-seven closure's 135 modules refusing): the unit of emission is the declaration, not the module. Phase B emits the declarations its tests reach, and each declaration's emitted text is a function of that declaration and its dependency closure only -- never of which tests demanded it, nor of which other declarations share its module or crate. A Rust module file is packaging: the reached declarations' texts in identity order; the file's bytes are not a key. Because other items in one crate can change how Rust compiles a declaration (a trait impl redirecting method resolution or inference, a colliding name, a glob import or other namespace influence, coherence, linkage), the crate's full declaration set stays in the realization product key unless the emitter structurally enforces composition independence, or the evidence admitting a narrower key carries its explicit bounded population. That evidence's falsifier builds a closure alone and beside adversarial co-residents (a redirecting trait impl, a colliding name, a glob import), and a mutation shows the key widening to the full declaration set when independence is unavailable; a passing pair authorizes sharing only for the population it carries. FOUR IDENTITIES, never collapsed into one verdict key: the EvaluationIdentity (the test judgment, its subject and context, semantic declared inputs, the semantic content identities of the reached declarations and the sealed read set, and the semantics version), so editing a dependency rekeys the evaluation; the RealizationProductIdentity (handler, generator, target model, toolchain, crate partition, build configuration, and the declaration set packaged into the product); the EvaluationResultBinding from EvaluationIdentity to ResultContentIdentity; and the realization receipt, which joins the three and records whether the product was served or rebuilt. Two admitted handlers therefore meet at one EvaluationIdentity and conflict when their content differs, and two tests that reach one declaration share its emitted text. Whole-module emission remains the self-hosting goal -- a module is self-emitted when every declaration in it emits -- and is not Phase B's bar. (e) GENERATOR: acceptance requires the v2 emitter, through the v2-native CLI (the second generation). There is no alternative to v2 emitting the native-seven test closure, because interpretation is not the route; seed emission is diagnostic evidence only, and generator identity is a key component so seed-emitted and v2-emitted products never collide. This puts the second generation on Phase B's critical path. (f) PRODUCT: the first attempt is an emitted Cargo test target executed by `cargo test`, normalized to per-test bindings through an adapter GENERATED from the test roster only where Cargo cannot carry the full result contract. No hand-written harness and no second evaluator; a generated verdict binary is admissible only if a feasibility receipt shows a Cargo test target cannot represent the contract. (g) REUSE: products may be reused across processes and runs. Making that safe is the design's obligation, and an anticipated safety issue changes the design rather than forcing a rebuild. This SUPERSEDES, as a principle, the operator's 2026-10-09 ruling recorded on `gunbc.emitted_subject_build_gate` (fresh emission and build of the retained products); its `build_fresh` switch flips in the change that makes reuse sound -- collision-resistant keys, binary payloads in the store, an observed store capacity, and receipts that distinguish served from rebuilt. Two products are reusable under their own complete keys: the native build artifact and the evaluation result bundle. A forced cold run is a falsifier -- periodic, and on any emitter, build-wiring, key-derivation or toolchain change -- never the normal mode. (h) NO FALLBACK: a construct the handler cannot realize refuses, typed and located; the route never invokes the interpreter or the seed, and acceptance includes a control with both absent. After D10 admission the interpreter is an offline differential oracle only. (i) EQUIVALENCE is full `JudgmentContract` equality (section 6), never Cargo's exit status or stdout alone. (j) TEXT: `v2.std.text` `String` (`FreeMonoid<Char>`) and the declared string-literal unfold are realized by the emitter's existing type realization; no runtime text representation is designed, and an executed non-ASCII control establishes Unicode-scalar behavior. Owner: roadmap node `native-test-verdict-realization`."),
      li(text: "**D16 -- Effects are ordered by access, never by position. Principle ruled 2026-10-18 (operator, session clever-ibex-310); five parts ruled 2026-10-25 (section 7.1).** RULED: section 2.4's rule -- `let` names and scopes and orders nothing; dependencies come from value data flow, selected alternatives, and the data flow of non-duplicable resource access; a conflict with no derived relation refuses. This supersedes, for the v2/native path, the remedy recorded on `gunbc.recurring_failure_mode` `sibling_operand_effect_order_is_underdetermined_and_unenforced` (rewrite into a `let`), whose finding that sibling position is no sequencing authority stands; v1's `SiblingOperandEffectOrderUndetermined` message stays truthful for the v1 path and is not reworded. RULED 2026-10-25 at the section 7.1 recommendations (operator, session clever-ibex-310), five parts: (1) the home of the access discipline; (2) non-duplication through closures, records, helpers and loops; (3) resource identity, and how a parent access splits and rejoins; (4) the continuation after failure or an uncertain outcome; (5) live, version-checked or retained-snapshot reads. Section 7.1 alone carries their rulings. Concurrent mergeable effects are out of scope until a law covering every promised observation and an implementation realizing it both exist. TRIGGER: the change that introduces the non-duplication check on the v2 acceptance path carries rulings on (1)-(5), and lands only after a census at function identity of the pairs it would refuse -- effect-reaching computations with no derived relation, re-derived by a `gunbc test` instrument rather than transcribed -- with every listed site rewritten or dispositioned, so the refusal is enforced from its first commit and never ships as a warning; until then section 2.4's effect rule is not enforced and no M2 effect specimen qualifies."),
      li(text: "**D17 -- Activation after an earlier decisive or refusing member in a pointwise operation. Deferred; trigger: the change that first lowers `any` or `all` off the carrier recurrence, or first lets a pointwise operation start a member before every earlier member's outcome is known.** When an early member decides `any` or `all`, is a later member whose predicate would refuse still demanded? When a `map` member refuses, are later members activated, and may their effects occur? Pure does not mean non-refusing, and Boolean associativity does not answer it. This is the operation's demand and failure contract, distinct from access admission (D16): two members with disjoint access can still differ in whether a later one runs after an earlier one refuses, and idempotency does not make an unowed write owed. Realization admission checks that an execution strategy preserves both. Option A: the decisive result wins and later members are not demanded. Option B: every member is demanded and the lowest-index refusal wins. The ruling either preserves what the current recurrence does or names the change. Until it is ruled, `any` and `all` stay on the recurrence, and no pointwise member starts before every earlier member's outcome is known."),
    ]),
    h3(text: "7.1 D16 parts (1)-(5): rulings (RULED 2026-10-25, operator, session clever-ibex-310; drafted by zesty-stag-143)"),
    p(text: "**Standing.** RULED (operator, 2026-10-25, session clever-ibex-310; drafted by zesty-stag-143): each part is ruled at its RECOMMENDATION. The other options stay as the record of what was weighed, marked not chosen, and each deferred next rung -- 2A, the 3B static splits beyond 3A, and the 3C dynamic identity route -- stays deferred with its trigger. Section 2.4's effect rule, D16's ruled principle, D16's TRIGGER and M2V's population gate (section M2V, Population and effects) are not reopened: the checker still lands carrying rulings on all five parts, after the census at function identity is dispositioned, refusing from its first commit; until then no effect is reordered and no M2 effect specimen qualifies. Nothing here declares a type. Where a recommendation needs a carrier the corpus lacks, it names a modeling obligation, and the change that discharges it extends the cited home rather than minting a parallel one (DESIGN section 3b)."),
    p(text: "**The cut holds, with a ruling order.** No part's ruling depended on another's being made first, but the rulings compose in one direction, so each states what it consumes and holds under every option of the parts it consumes: (1) the home comes first; (3) identity fixes what one access is about; (2) non-duplication is checked per (3)-subject over the concrete access instances (1) places in the plan; (4) fixes the continuation every effect yields; (5) consumes (3) for what a snapshot or version is of and (4) for what a failed version check yields. A checker that carries (2) without (3) is still sound, since it reads every subject as one atomic resource, which over-refuses; that coarse reading is option 3A below, not a second design."),
    p(text: "**What the B0 census bears on.** The instrument is `gunbc test //gunbc/instruments:effect-pair-census` (`v2.compiler.effect_pair_census`); its counts are re-derived by that run and not carried here (DESIGN section 6). Its classes map onto the parts as follows. Pair rows (`EffectPairRow`) bear on part (3) first: their `SharedPathStanding` is a declared-path reading -- `DistinctDeclaredPaths` never means independent, and `AliasUnknown` is part (3)'s unmodelled subject identity -- which is why the census over-counts; after (3), the remainder is what part (1)'s footprint and part (2)'s check judge. Rows reached through `ReachesFunctionValueCall` and the `[effect-pair-repetition-unknown]` rows (`FactRepetition` `LambdaInstantiationUnknown`) bear on part (2)'s closure clause. The `[effect-pair-repeated]` rows (`LoopIteratedBody`) bear on part (2)'s loop clause and part (3)'s per-member split. The `[effect-pair-unclassified]` rows bear on part (1): they are M2V's modeling obligation (operation identity has two keyings), and part (1)'s recommendation discharges it with the same declaration. Rows reached through `ReachesOpaqueOperation` or `ReachesUndeclaredOperation` are already ruled (an opaque call couples conservatively; silence is unknown, D13). The `[effect-pair-not-derived]` and `[effect-pair-activation-not-derived]` rows bear on none of (1)-(5): they are completeness gaps of the existing derivations (`v2.compiler.value_use_dependency` `NotDerivedReason`, `v2.compiler.activation_dependency` `ActivationNotDerivedReason`), which the checker refuses as typed unresolved dispositions before judging any pair, and which M2V repairs at their derivations. Parts (4) and (5) have no census class: the census reads no outcome contract and no read class, so their populations are the operations that declare (or fail to declare) those contracts, measured when the declarations exist."),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "**D16(1): access homes — 1A ruled 2026-10-25, clever-ibex-310 (draft zesty-stag-143).** D13's v2.compiler.dependency_demand fixed point must derive a parametric footprint per declaration: subject references relative to formal/captured/environmental binding slots, std.effects.EffectShape and shared-capable/exclusive mode. DemandUndecided remains absorbing. Instantiate that template against actual call-site bindings without inlining or another call walk; unresolved bindings refuse. Only M2V's total plan owns concrete accesses, phase boundaries, continuations, conflicts and prerequisite order, through v2.compiler.value_use_dependency and activation_dependency. D13 retains no ordering sidecar; its ResourceSet remains a projection. Each capability declares its EffectShape beside resource requirement/execution semantics, keyed by resolved declaration path, discharging M2V's two-keyings obligation. ResourceMode is the resource ceiling: an exceeding shape refuses. Missing operation shape is typed unknown, never read-only or no-access. OwnershipMode/RequiresAccessWitness become projections of checked relations; v2.lens.ownership is not their producer. std.resources.ResourceHandle remains the grant/realization carrier (cap: Secret), never a compiler-threaded or authored access token. **Still unmodeled:** parametric footprints, call-site instantiation and per-capability shape declarations; concrete access/prerequisite rows must enter the existing plan's generic relations. Rejected alternatives and rationale remain in the pinned original."),
      li(text: "**D16(2): non-duplication — 2B ruled 2026-10-25; 2A is the next rung.** Charge each helper's instantiated D13 footprint at its actual bindings: copy(a,a) conflicts; copy(a,b) also conflicts unless (3) proves disjointness. Admit effect-reaching lambdas only at statically known template positions (map/fold argument or immediate application). Stored, returned or function-value-called effectful lambdas and unknown repetition remain typed, located Undecided refusals. A fold/Loop member follows its predecessor's access only when the carried value it consumes has a derived value-use dependency on the predecessor's effect result/completion evidence. An independently available carrier orders nothing. Never add iteration-position edges, infer sequential intent from conflicts or thread access through a carrier. Unordered conflicting map/fold/Loop members on an undivided subject refuse; proven per-member disjointness may admit them. Close every shared read phase before the next exclusive use. D13 owns access mode, not phase; phase opening/closing is order in the one plan, not another type. **Next-rung trigger:** inferred function types carry footprints through stored/passed/returned closures; application consumes the footprint and record construction checks aliases. Until that capability exists, no widening admits the refused closures. D4 supplies static templates with runtime membership, D13 permits closure conversion, and D17 governs pointwise activation after refusal; no blanket prohibition on every effectful lambda/repeated body is adopted."),
      li(text: "**D16(3): subject identity — 3A first, then 3B; 3C deferred, ruled 2026-10-25.** Initially treat each D13 logical subject instance as atomic. Two accesses to the same subject conflict when either is exclusive. Composition-root binding must establish disjointness, overlap or alias-unknown; only established disjointness permits independence. Otherwise treat as one atomic subject or refuse. Existing derived order can admit conflicting same-subject accesses; the checker never chooses an order, even for different file paths. **3B:** refine to (subject, std.effect_grant.NamespacePosition, facet), using the cited containment law and its convergence onto one containment walk. EffectShape derives content for Read/Upsert/Append, parent entry-set for Create/Delete and potentially creating Upsert. Split exclusive parent access only on static disjointness evidence and provider-declared namespace injectivity; symlinks, hard links, case folding and mounts invalidate path-spelling inference. Rejoin consumes every child continuation, whose product forms the parent's continuation. **3C trigger:** a population needs runtime identity. A declared observation demand precedes either conflicting effect, yields identity/alias/unknown, then admits subject instances and instantiates existing relations; realization-local conflict selection remains forbidden. **Still unmodeled:** binding disjoint/overlap/unknown verdicts and namespace injectivity at the provider binding; metadata-read and opaque ExecuteEffect footprints at std.effects.EffectShape or the operation declaration, not a parallel facet type. Opaque execution couples conservatively until declared. Facet derivation lands with 3B, not earlier."),
      li(text: "**D16(4): outcome-indexed continuation — 4A ruled 2026-10-25.** Each declared operation outcome maps independently to exactly one continuation: predecessor only when nothing was applied (CasPreconditionFailed/NotApplied); committed/post-state when a known state is established, including known partial/failure states; otherwise uncertain. A refusal after an effect is not rollback. Without an outcome contract every non-success arm is uncertain. Uncertain continuation permits observation or an explicitly licensed retry, never a presumed predecessor/post-state. D14 ensure is its standard recovery consumer, not its only permitted observation. The retry/uncertain-completion contract must bind operands, std.temporal_effect.EffectAttemptIntent identity, dedup/fencing, result/refusal reconciliation and receipt multiplicity; every attempt appears in execution evidence. std.effects.IdempotencyEvidence alone licenses nothing: convergence is not result equivalence. A realization never retries on its own. DemandNature supplies neither retry permission nor veto: FreshEffect forbids sharing/memoizing/collapsing distinct semantic occurrences, while even a fresh effect can retry the same semantic attempt under the declared contract. **Still unmodeled:** applied-unknown in std.realization_reconcile.Application; per-operation outcome-to-continuation declaration beside the access shape; retry contract extending EffectAttemptIntent with IdempotencyEvidence as input, never a parallel attempt type. Checker refuses predecessor use without nothing-applied evidence, unlicensed uncertain-state operations and idempotency-only retries."),
      li(text: "**D16(5): declared read classes — 5A ruled 2026-10-25; LIVE default.** LIVE consumes the subject's current continuation in a shared read phase, after its last exclusive access and before the next. Serving any prior observation requires matching external-observation identity, read contract, std.materialization_ladder.WorldRead admission under its staleness envelope and retention obligation; ResourceKind.Observation proves neither freshness nor replay. VERSION-CHECKED returns a version witness with the value; a conditional write uses CAS and its precondition-failed continuation from (4). Refuse a version condition without a prior read of the same (3)-subject. RETAINED-SNAPSHOT uses a store keyed by subject/version whose retention spans every consumer, and imposes no subsequent order; refuse insufficient retention. A retained immutable read result already retains its bytes, but version numbers, hashes and lease epochs alone do not. **Still unmodeled:** one per-read contract on the operation beside its access shape and outcome contract; reuse existing ladder/store, CasExpectation/CasSlotVersion, temporal LeaseEpoch/BaseSnapshot and EvidenceFreshness authorities. Live-only/no-reuse and version-checked-by-default were not selected."),
    ]),
  ]
}

data demand_engine_program_plan: Plan = Plan {
  projection: PlanIsAuthorityOnly { ruling: project_documentation_projections_are_not_checkout_artifacts },
  slug: "demand-engine-program",
  title: "Demand-engine program: derived demand, lawful decomposition, realization and materialization",
  body: demand_engine_program_body(),
  retirement: PlanRetiresWhen { condition: unbound_dissolution(description: "Delete this document when M7 exit holds and every earlier milestone's deletions are census-verified: program runs go through the demand engine, enrolled native-fold production keys inhabit named relations, no production dependency relation restates derivable program flow, no feature-authored concurrency width, environment knob, shard count, minimum-one fallback or refusal-to-serial path remains (M5), the store serves cross-run repeats on the landing-receipt path (M5), on an admitted warm-materialization path recomputation is limited to changed evaluation identities and their declared transitive dependents plus explicitly classified fresh/global obligations (M6), and every production decomposition cites law evidence (M7) -- at which point the carriers state the design and this document is a second copy of it. -- and only once D9 and D10 are terminal: D9 terminal means the emitted-reference edges are provenance-classified, the admitted candidate A/B has produced SelectedWithin, and the selected RustCratePartitionIdentity (including a selected one-crate baseline when that wins) is carried in build_configuration; D10 terminal means NativeProductionAdmitted holds for the named required denominator, every demanded subject has a total attributed outcome, no contract-violating acceptance or required-subject refusal remains, unattributed refusals are zero, every v1/v2 divergence is dispositioned against the independent authority, and the interpreter fallback route is deleted with the post-cut red/revert latch live; a terminal NativeProductionRefused is evidence and never dissolves this plan; D11 terminal per its row -- explicit-contract callability implemented, one admitted native handler per in-denominator effect, typed refusals for unsupported effects, displaced direct driver implementations deleted, no-interpreter controls executing on the production route") }
}
